{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018nwU6iL5XWoLHdBgBUo5Qv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RPBWdtYMC2geJnrWx5QpvE","name":"ToolSearch","input":{"query":"select:mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","max_results":1},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":25155,"cache_read_input_tokens":22909,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":25155},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b19c43c-1b27-4951-bda8-40543516a891","uuid":"1f0f39a8-4d79-4870-89f2-3b3a2bfbc9f3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_01RPBWdtYMC2geJnrWx5QpvE","content":[{"type":"tool_reference","tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b19c43c-1b27-4951-bda8-40543516a891","uuid":"7e124d78-60e1-4209-8a2b-7810ea114704","timestamp":"2026-05-14T19:25:03.616Z","tool_use_result":{"matches":["mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report"],"query":"select:mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","total_deferred_tools":23}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011rGGPmz1y6rZfSztnr52oE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, the tool is loaded. Now I'll dispatch two reviewers in parallel:\n\n- R1: Methods + Experiments focus\n- R2: Novelty + Related Work focus\n\nI need to give each reviewer comprehensive context about the paper since they can't see the PDF images. Let me write detailed prompts that include the paper content.\n\nLet me structure the paper content I've extracted:\n\n**Title**: \"When Labeling Functions Lie: Circular Evaluation Traps in Weak Supervision for Agent Monitoring\"\n\n**Abstract**: Detecting progress stagnation in LLM agents is critical. They use weak supervision with heuristic LFs (command repetition, file modification, outcome backpropagation). \n\nThe core problem is that these heuristic labeling functions create a circular trap—models achieve near-perfect performance (0.995 F1) when evaluated against the same LF-derived labels they were trained on, but this masks a fundamental mismatch with actual stagnation. When tested on a 650-window gold standard, the labeling functions only agree with human judgment at κ=0.38 and miss nearly half of true stagnation cases. All models then collapse to a narrow 0.55-0.60 performance band on the gold set, revealing that their apparent success was just fitting the biased heuristics. The temporal dynamics of the labeling functions actually diagnose their own failure modes rather than capturing when stagnation truly begins, and temporal label models can't salvage fundamentally flawed labeling functions.\n\nThe main contributions are identifying this circular evaluation trap (the 0.995 to 0.58 performance cliff), demonstrating the severity of label bias, and showing why standard weak supervision approaches fail here. Looking at the performance collapse across different model architectures and embedding approaches, I'm seeing a consistent pattern where models trained on the noisy labels achieve near-perfect accuracy but then drop dramatically when evaluated on gold annotations—gradient boosting falls from 99.5% to 58%, random forests from 99.5% to 55%, and logistic regression from 98.7% to 61%. When I retrain gradient boosting on the gold set directly, performance recovers to 63%. The embedding models show similar degradation, with Qwen, E5, and BGE all dropping by roughly 33-35 percentage points. Across all models tested on gold labels, performance converges around 55-60%, and this pattern holds even when comparing across different agents on the benchmark.\n\nNow I'm examining the failure modes more closely—there are five distinct categories of errors, with semantic diversity being the most common failure type but also the hardest to detect, while clear structural loops are rare but the model catches them reliably. The per-subtype performance varies dramatically, with structural loops achieving strong F1 scores around 0.75-0.82 across different settings, whereas semantic-diverse failures consistently underperform at 0.25-0.38. Looking at the temporal dynamics, the onset of label noise doesn't align cleanly with when the model starts failing—in about a third of cases the noise leads the failure, another third it lags, and the rest are roughly synchronized with an average offset of about 3.8 steps.\n\nThe correlations with command repetition, file diffs, and outcome backpropagation are all quite weak, suggesting these surface-level signals don't capture what's really driving the failures. The majority voting baseline on the gold set achieves 62% accuracy with moderate agreement, while the more sophisticated approaches like HMM variants don't improve much, though accounting for temporal lag helps slightly. The core insight emerging is that the research community should shift focus from architectural improvements to the quality of the labels themselves, and the limitations here—a relatively small gold set of 650 examples, focus only on software engineering tasks with two specific agents, and lack of inter-annotator agreement metrics—suggest these findings need validation across broader domains.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the experimental coverage across the paper sections. The gold set experiments span from agreement metrics through structural feature analysis, probe-based approaches, LLM judges, temporal alignment, failure taxonomies, and cross-agent comparisons, with each one anchored to specific sections and tables throughout the work.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1166,"cache_read_input_tokens":48064,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1166},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b19c43c-1b27-4951-bda8-40543516a891","uuid":"12d44f1f-1cdd-4aa2-a90a-7f3768dd7801"}
