{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SCEuB8M4YtjKEQBVeZv9af","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260514T184058\n3\ttier: median\n4\toverall_score: weak reject\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-14T19:24:16.356152+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report — agent_progress_probe\n9\t\n10\t**Paper**: \"Structural Features Dominate Current Approaches to Progress Stagnation Detection in LLM Agents: A Systematic Empirical Study with Human Validation\"\n11\t\n12\t**Review Mode**: Full (2-reviewer roundtable)\n13\t**Venue Standard**: Top ML venue, ~25% acceptance rate, 6-point scale\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## Roundtable Summary\n18\t\n19\tBoth reviewers independently scored 3 (Weak Reject) with confidence 4/5. Cross-sharing revealed strong convergence on core assessments: the circular evaluation contribution is genuinely valuable, but the paper's central claim (structural dominance) is insufficiently supported by the statistical evidence provided, and novelty is below the top-venue threshold. No score changes after discussion.\n20\t\n21\t**Consensus Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n22\t\n23\t---\n24\t\n25\t## R1 — Methods and Experiments\n26\t\n27\t### Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5\n28\t\n29\t### Strengths\n30\t\n31\t- **Circularity diagnosis is the paper's strongest contribution.** Quantifying the ~22pp inflation from LF-eval to gold-eval (structural GB: 0.995 → 0.76) is a genuine methodological service. The explanation — that structural features reverse-engineer the LF voting logic — is clear and well-supported by the data.\n32\t\n33\t- **Systematic multi-method comparison on a common gold set.** Evaluating structural classifiers, three neural probe encoders, and LLM-as-judge all on the same 650-window gold set with consistent metrics is methodologically clean.\n34\t\n35\t- **Honest negative results on temporal label models.** The TWV/HMM/lag-aware HMM failure is well-documented with informative failure analysis (low-conflict 3-LF regime leaves no room for temporal re-weighting).\n36\t\n37\t- **Temporal dynamics analysis with gold validation.** The 52% onset alignment (within ±2 steps) against gold-annotated boundaries provides partial independent validation beyond circular analysis.\n38\t\n39\t- **Semantic residual taxonomy.** Decomposing the 28.9% of missed gold stagnation into strategic dead-ends (11.5%), implicit repetition (8.9%), and unfocused exploration (8.5%) is descriptively useful.\n40\t\n41\t### Weaknesses\n42\t\n43\t- **No confidence intervals or significance tests on any metric.** The core claim rests on a 4pp gap between GB (0.76) and BGE-m3 probe (0.72) on 650 windows. Without bootstrap CIs or McNemar's test, it is impossible to assess whether this gap is statistically meaningful. This is a serious omission for an empirical study.\n44\t\n45\t- **Gold set quality concerns.** κ=0.67 falls below the 0.80 threshold typically expected for reliable ground truth. With ~33% of disagreements attributable to noise, the gold-derived performance numbers carry substantial measurement error. Sub-analyses on small samples (e.g., n=23 for unfocused exploration, n=42 for OB-dominant windows) lack statistical power.\n46\t\n47\t- **Unfair LLM-judge baseline.** Only zero-shot prompting was tested. Even a simple 5-10 shot prompt with gold examples could substantially improve LLM performance. The 16pp gap (0.76 vs 0.60) may partly reflect prompting quality rather than fundamental capability limits. Without few-shot or chain-of-thought variants, the comparison is incomplete.\n48\t\n49\t- **Structural models trained on LF-derived labels evaluated on gold.** This partially breaks the circularity (evaluation is independent) but training signal is still circular. A fairer test would include gold-label cross-validation (5-fold on 650 samples), even if sample size is limiting. The paper does not attempt this.\n50\t\n51\t- **Semantic residual analysis is post-hoc and qualitative.** The three categories emerge from manual inspection of 78 windows with no inter-annotator agreement reported for the taxonomy itself, no formal coding scheme, and no quantitative features distinguishing the categories. The F1 ≤ 0.38 numbers lack methodological detail.\n52\t\n53\t- **Cross-agent claims lack gold validation.** The OpenHands structural F1 of 0.967 is evaluated on LF-derived labels — exactly the circularity the gold set was meant to break. Given known cmd_rep issues on OpenHands (92.3% firing rate, 71% FP rate), the circularity gap transfer assumption is untested and potentially dubious.\n54\t\n55\t- **TLM evaluation is confounded.** TWV cross-agent degradation of -21.7pp is measured against MV labels. If TWV labels are actually closer to gold truth than MV, evaluating against MV penalizes TWV. Without evaluating TWV-trained models on gold labels, the negative finding is not fully convincing.\n56\t\n57\t- **52% temporal onset alignment is weak and lacks a null baseline.** Nearly half of onset events are misaligned by >2 steps. No random-firing null distribution is provided to calibrate whether 52% exceeds chance expectation.\n58\t\n59\t### Key Questions\n60\t\n61\t1. Can you provide bootstrap 95% CIs for the GB vs. BGE-m3 gap (4pp on gold)? Is this significant under McNemar's test?\n62\t2. Why was only zero-shot LLM-judge tested? Have you considered few-shot variants?\n63\t3. Did you attempt gold-label training (5-fold CV on 650 gold samples) to separate training circularity from evaluation circularity?\n64\t4. What is the expected alignment percentage under a random-firing null baseline?\n65\t5. What inter-rater agreement was achieved on the semantic residual three-way taxonomy?\n66\t\n67\t---\n68\t\n69\t## R2 — Novelty and Related Work\n70\t\n71\t### Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5\n72\t\n73\t### Strengths\n74\t\n75\t- **Circular evaluation framework has genuine transferable value.** The demonstration that LF-derived evaluation inflates structural classifier performance by ~22pp, with a clear mechanistic explanation (features ≈ LF inputs), generalizes as a cautionary finding for any weak supervision pipeline with correlated features.\n76\t\n77\t- **Practical relevance is clear and well-articulated.** Structural classifiers at <5ms/window outperforming $0.006/window LLM-judge by 16pp is a useful deployment finding.\n78\t\n79\t- **Cross-agent cmd_rep role reversal is an interesting empirical finding.** The architecture-dependent temporal behavior (leading on SWE-Agent, lagging on OpenHands, removal improving OH F1 by +21.6pp) reveals meaningful agent-architecture interactions.\n80\t\n81\t- **Well-documented negative results.** The TLM failure analysis correctly identifies the low-conflict regime as the root cause, providing useful constraints for future temporal label model research.\n82\t\n83\t### Weaknesses\n84\t\n85\t- **Core finding is largely expected given the experimental design.** Structural features are literally the inputs to the LFs that generate training labels. Even after gold validation reduces the gap, the finding that structural features dominate is more of a systems confirmation than a scientific discovery. The paper does not adequately reckon with the tautological aspect — human annotators may also anchor on the same structural signals (command repetition, file inactivity) that the LFs encode.\n86\t\n87\t- **\"Semantic residual\" concept has limited novelty.** The observation that programmatic labeling functions miss cases requiring deeper semantic understanding is a well-known limitation of data programming (Ratner et al. 2016, Zhang et al. 2021 WRENCH). The stagnation subtype taxonomy is descriptively useful but does not constitute a novel conceptual contribution.\n88\t\n89\t- **Significant related work gaps:**\n90\t  - **Data programming beyond Snorkel**: FlyingSquid (Fu et al., ICML 2020), WRENCH benchmark (Zhang et al., NeurIPS 2021), Snorkel MeTaL (Ratner et al. 2019) — all directly relevant to the label model comparison.\n91\t  - **Sequential weak supervision**: Safranchik et al. (AAAI 2020, linked HMM for sequence tagging), Sala et al. (NeurIPS 2019, multi-resolution WS) — the most direct methodological predecessors for the temporal label model attempt. Their omission prevents the paper from claiming novelty on sequential data programming.\n92\t  - **Process supervision**: Lightman et al. (2023, \"Let's Verify Step by Step\"), Math-Shepherd (Wang et al. 2024) — foundational PRM works predating the cited AgentPRM.\n93\t  - **Crowdsourcing with temporal structure**: Nguyen et al. (ACL 2017, HMM-Crowd) — directly relevant to the HMM-TLM approach.\n94\t  - **Agent trajectory analysis**: Runtime verification, test-time compute scaling (Snell et al. 2024) — broader context for agent monitoring.\n95\t\n96\t- **Negative TLM result is expected in the chosen regime.** With only 3 LFs and 95.1% agreement at conf≥2, there is essentially nothing for a temporal model to fix. The paper acknowledges this but doesn't adequately justify why this regime was chosen over a more informative 6-8 LF setup. The WRENCH benchmark typically uses 10+ LFs.\n97\t\n98\t- **Positioning for a top ML venue is uncertain.** The transferable methodological insights (circular evaluation framework, temporal onset validation) are solid workshop-level contributions. The domain-specific findings (structural dominance for SWE-bench coding agents) may not generalize to other agent types (web navigation, scientific discovery). The contribution bundle does not individually or collectively reach the acceptance threshold for a long paper.\n99\t\n100\t- **Reference list is thin.** Only 10 references in the paper. For an empirical study positioning against multiple research areas (weak supervision, agent monitoring, process reward models), this is insufficient to demonstrate command of the literature.\n101\t\n102\t### Key Questions\n103\t\n104\t1. How do you distinguish \"structural features dominate\" from the tautological observation that features correlated with label-generating heuristics predict those labels? Can you rule out that gold annotations are also anchored on structural signals?\n105\t2. Why were only 3 LFs used, given the negative TLM result is attributed to the low-conflict regime? Would 6-8 LFs produce a more informative test?\n106\t3. Why are FlyingSquid, Safranchik 2020, WRENCH, and Nguyen 2017 omitted from Related Work despite being directly relevant?\n107\t4. Does the circular evaluation finding generalize beyond this specific setup (3 LFs, structural features, coding agents)?\n108\t5. Would this paper be better positioned as a workshop paper focused on the circular evaluation methodology?\n109\t\n110\t---\n111\t\n112\t## Roundtable Convergence\n113\t\n114\t### Areas of Strong Agreement\n115\t\n116\t1. **Circular evaluation is the best contribution.** Both reviewers identify the ~22pp inflation quantification and its mechanistic explanation as the paper's most transferable insight.\n117\t2. **Missing significance tests are a critical gap.** The 4pp core claim (GB 0.76 vs BGE-m3 0.72) cannot be evaluated without CIs or hypothesis tests on a 650-sample gold set.\n118\t3. **Unfair LLM-judge baseline.** Zero-shot only; few-shot would be a minimal fair comparison.\n119\t4. **Semantic residual has limited novelty.** Descriptively useful but not a new conceptual contribution — it restates known weak supervision limitations.\n120\t5. **Gold set limitations.** κ=0.67 is below standard thresholds; sub-analyses on small subgroups lack power.\n121\t6. **Cross-agent claims are unvalidated.** OpenHands evaluation remains on LF-derived labels.\n122\t7. **Negative TLM result is weakened by the low-conflict regime.** Both reviewers see this as somewhat expected rather than surprising.\n123\t\n124\t### Nuanced Divergences\n125\t\n126\t- R1 emphasizes the **TLM evaluation confound** (evaluating against MV rather than gold) more strongly; R2 emphasizes the **regime choice** (3 LFs insufficient for a meaningful test).\n127\t- R2 provides a more extensive **missing related work** analysis, identifying 8+ directly relevant omitted papers.\n128\t- R1 raises the **temporal onset null baseline** issue more prominently; R2 focuses more on the **tautological nature** of the structural dominance claim.\n129\t\n130\t### Consensus Recommendations\n131\t\n132\t**For the authors if revising:**\n133\t1. Add bootstrap 95% CIs and McNemar's tests for all pairwise comparisons on the gold set.\n134\t2. Include few-shot (5-10 examples) and CoT LLM-judge baselines.\n135\t3. Attempt gold-label cross-validation for structural classifiers.\n136\t4. Provide a random-firing null baseline for temporal onset alignment.\n137\t5. Expand related work substantially: FlyingSquid, Safranchik 2020, WRENCH, Lightman 2023, Sala 2019, Nguyen 2017.\n138\t6. Report inter-rater agreement on the semantic residual taxonomy.\n139\t7. Consider OpenHands gold annotation (even a small sample) to validate cross-agent claims.\n140\t8. Increase LF count (6-8) to provide a more informative test of temporal label models.\n141\t\n142\t**Venue recommendation:** The circular evaluation framework and the structural-vs-semantic comparison would make a strong workshop paper (NeurIPS Agents Workshop, EMNLP Findings). For a main conference long paper, the novelty and statistical rigor need significant strengthening.\n143\t\n144\t---\n145\t\n146\t## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n147\t\n148\tAll 8 imagined experiments from the review context are represented in the paper:\n149\t\n150\t| Experiment | Coverage | Notes |\n151\t|---|---|---|\n152\t| gold-annotation-stats | ✓ Table 1 | Complete |\n153\t| gold-lf-validation | ✓ Tables 2-3 | Complete |\n154\t| gold-structural-eval | ✓ Table 4 | Complete |\n155\t| gold-probe-eval | ✓ Table 5 | Complete |\n156\t| gold-llm-judge-eval | ✓ Table 6 | Complete |\n157\t| gold-temporal-validation | ✓ Table 7 | Complete |\n158\t| gold-error-taxonomy | ✓ Table 8 | Complete |\n159\t| f1-stag-per-class-breakdown | ✓ Table 9 | Complete |\n160\t\n161\tCoverage is comprehensive — the paper reports all planned experiments with appropriate tables.\n162\t\n163\t---\n164\t\n165\t## Final Verdict\n166\t\n167\t**Overall Score: 3 — Weak Reject**\n168\t\n169\tThe paper addresses a timely problem (stagnation detection in LLM agents) with a reasonable experimental methodology. The circular evaluation contribution is genuinely valuable and the gold set validation is the right approach. However, the core \"structural dominance\" finding carries a tautological flavor that the paper does not adequately address, the statistical evidence lacks rigor (no CIs, no significance tests, κ=0.67 gold set), the LLM baseline comparison is unfair, the related work has significant gaps, and the negative TLM result is weakened by the low-conflict regime. For a top venue at ~25% acceptance, the novelty and evidence bars are not met in current form. With statistical rigor improvements, fairer baselines, and tighter positioning (perhaps as Findings or a workshop paper), this could become a solid contribution."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01YCtSqhgHHVtRnbSQqtrnQK","session_id":"0b19c43c-1b27-4951-bda8-40543516a891","uuid":"2ab53b96-684f-4183-94d1-637415752eb0","timestamp":"2026-05-14T19:26:52.695Z"}
