{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014YnoRqaqXNjExvytgfB15D","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n10\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n11\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n12\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n13\t\n14\t\\title{Structural Features Dominate Current Approaches to\\\\Progress Stagnation Detection in LLM Agents:\\\\A Systematic Empirical Study with Human Validation}\n15\t\n16\t\\author{Anonymous}\n17\t\n18\t\\date{}\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{document}\n21\t\n22\t\\maketitle\n23\t\n24\t%==============================================================================\n25\t\\begin{abstract}\n26\t%==============================================================================\n27\t\n28\tLLM-based agents frequently enter \\emph{progress stagnation}---sustained periods of goal-irrelevant activity that waste compute and degrade task outcomes. We present a systematic empirical study of stagnation detection through weak supervision on 54,809 trajectory windows from two coding agents (SWE-Agent and OpenHands). Our central finding, validated on a 650-window human-annotated gold set ($\\kappa\\!=\\!0.67$), is that \\textbf{structural features dominate all current automated approaches}: a Gradient Boosted classifier over 30 lightweight features (command repetition ratio, file diff frequency, unique command fraction) achieves macro-F1\\,=\\,0.76 on gold labels, outperforming neural embedding probes (0.69--0.72) and an LLM-as-judge baseline (0.60). However, gold evaluation reveals that labeling-function-derived labels inflate performance by $\\sim$22 percentage points through circular evaluation, and that 28.9\\% of human-recognized stagnation constitutes a \\emph{semantic residual}---strategic dead-ends, implicit repetition, and unfocused exploration---that no current method captures effectively ($\\text{F1}\\!\\leq\\!0.38$). We further show that labeling functions exhibit systematic but agent-specific temporal dynamics, with onset alignment to human annotations at 52\\% within $\\pm$2 steps---a statistical trend, not a precise predictor. Finally, three temporal label model implementations (TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM) all fail to improve over majority vote, with TWV degrading downstream classifiers by up to 21.7pp. Our results redirect agent monitoring research from expensive representation learning toward lightweight structural approaches, while identifying semantic stagnation as a concrete open challenge.\n29\t\n30\t\\end{abstract}\n31\t\n32\t%==============================================================================\n33\t\\section{Introduction}\n34\t\\label{sec:intro}\n35\t%==============================================================================\n36\t\n37\tLLM-based agents deployed on complex software engineering tasks---such as those benchmarked by SWE-bench---routinely enter prolonged episodes of unproductive behavior. An agent may issue diverse, syntactically varied commands that individually appear reasonable yet collectively make no progress toward the goal. This \\emph{progress stagnation} phenomenon wastes compute, delays task resolution, and represents a key bottleneck for autonomous agent deployments.\n38\t\n39\tExisting detection approaches fall into three broad categories, each with significant limitations. \\emph{Cycle detectors} identify literal command or state repetition but miss semantically diverse stagnation. \\emph{Process reward models} (PRMs) provide step-level value estimates but require outcome labels and RL training pipelines, positioning them as training signals rather than monitoring tools. \\emph{Self-reflection methods} (Reflexion, LATS) rely on the stagnating agent's own judgment---precisely the capability that has failed.\n40\t\n41\tWe present a systematic empirical study of stagnation detection through weak supervision. Using three labeling functions (LFs)---command repetition, file diff inactivity, and outcome backpropagation---we label 54,809 trajectory windows from SWE-Agent and OpenHands, then train and compare structural classifiers, neural embedding probes, and an LLM-as-judge baseline. Crucially, we validate all findings against a 650-window human-annotated gold set.\n42\t\n43\tOur study yields three contributions:\n44\t\n45\t\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]\n46\t\n47\t\\item \\textbf{Structural features dominate current approaches} (\\S\\ref{sec:structural-eval}). On gold labels, a Gradient Boosted structural classifier (macro-F1\\,=\\,0.76) outperforms the best neural probe (0.72, BGE-m3 encoder) by 4pp and an LLM-as-judge (0.60) by 16pp. LF-approximated stagnation is structural in nature; however, human-recognizable stagnation has additional semantic dimensions that no current automated method captures. Gold evaluation further reveals that LF-derived evaluation inflates reported performance by $\\sim$22pp through circular evaluation---a methodological finding with implications for any weak supervision study on sequential data.\n48\t\n49\t\\item \\textbf{LF temporal dynamics are systematic but noisy} (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal}). LFs exhibit agent-specific temporal roles: on SWE-Agent, \\texttt{cmd\\_rep} leads stagnation onset by 1.2 steps (gold-validated) while \\texttt{outcome\\_bp} lags by 5.3 steps. Onset alignment with human annotations reaches 52\\% within $\\pm$2 steps---a useful statistical trend ($\\pm$2--3 step noise) but not a precise intervention trigger.\n50\t\n51\t\\item \\textbf{Temporal label models fail to improve over majority vote} (\\S\\ref{sec:tlm}). Three implementations (TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM) all fail to surpass simple majority vote, with TWV labels degrading downstream structural classifiers by up to 21.7pp in cross-agent transfer. This well-motivated negative finding constrains future temporal label model research by identifying the low-conflict regime ($\\geq$95\\% agreement at conf$\\geq$2) as a key limiting factor.\n52\t\n53\t\\end{enumerate}\n54\t\n55\t%==============================================================================\n56\t\\section{Related Work}\n57\t\\label{sec:related}\n58\t%==============================================================================\n59\t\n60\t\\paragraph{Agent Failure Detection.}\n61\tCycle detection methods (CDCS+CDSA, SpecRA, TrajAD) identify repetitive or anomalous agent states but miss semantically diverse stagnation where commands vary in surface form. SlopCodeBench quantifies coding agent degradation over long trajectories, reporting verbosity increases in 90\\% of trajectories and quality erosion in 80\\%, motivating the need for real-time monitoring.\n62\t\n63\t\\paragraph{Process Reward Models.}\n64\tAgentPRM trains step-level PRMs using TD/GAE to evaluate ``promise and progress,'' but requires outcome labels and RL infrastructure. Our approach differs fundamentally: we use weak supervision (no outcome labels), operate at the window level (capturing temporal dynamics), and target monitoring rather than training.\n65\t\n66\t\\paragraph{Weak Supervision on Sequential Data.}\n67\tThe data programming paradigm, originating from Snorkel, assumes conditionally independent labeling functions. Agent trajectories violate this assumption: LFs fire in systematic temporal patterns along trajectories. Our study empirically characterizes this violation and tests whether temporal label models can exploit it.\n68\t\n69\t\\paragraph{Self-Reflection and LLM-as-Judge.}\n70\tReflexion, LATS, and Tree of Thoughts use LLM self-evaluation for course correction. These are intervention mechanisms, not monitoring tools, and rely on the stagnating agent's own (degraded) judgment. LLM-as-judge approaches offer external evaluation but at high computational cost.\n71\t\n72\t%==============================================================================\n73\t\\section{Problem Setting and Data}\n74\t\\label{sec:data}\n75\t%==============================================================================\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{Task and Agents.}\n78\tWe study SWE-bench Verified trajectories from two architecturally distinct coding agents: SWE-Agent (command-line interaction, structured action space) and OpenHands (IDE-based, broader action repertoire). Trajectories are segmented into overlapping windows of $k\\!=\\!5$ consecutive steps.\n79\t\n80\t\\paragraph{Labeling Functions.}\n81\tThree LFs provide complementary stagnation signals:\n82\t\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=1pt]\n83\t\\item \\texttt{cmd\\_rep}: Command repetition ratio within the window, thresholded.\n84\t\\item \\texttt{file\\_diff}: File modification inactivity---no meaningful file changes across the window.\n85\t\\item \\texttt{outcome\\_bp}: Outcome backpropagation---stagnation label propagated backward from unsuccessful task completion.\n86\t\\end{itemize}\n87\tMajority vote (MV) over these three LFs produces the training label. Each window receives a confidence score (1--3) equal to the number of agreeing LFs. The full dataset comprises 54,809 labeled windows. At conf$\\geq$2, the LF agreement rate is 95.1\\%.\n88\t\n89\t\\paragraph{Gold Annotation.}\n90\t\\label{sec:gold-annotation}\n91\tWe construct a 650-window gold set via stratified sampling (Table~\\ref{tab:gold-stats}). Two annotators independently label each window; a third adjudicates disagreements ($n\\!=\\!124$, 19.1\\%). Inter-annotator agreement reaches $\\kappa\\!=\\!0.67$ (substantial), with disagreements concentrated on edge cases: ineffective edits and late-trajectory exploratory attempts.\n92\t\n93\t\\begin{table}[t]\n94\t\\centering\n95\t\\small\n96\t\\caption{Gold set annotation summary.}\n97\t\\label{tab:gold-stats}\n98\t\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n99\t\\toprule\n100\tMetric & Value \\\\\n101\t\\midrule\n102\tTotal windows & 650 \\\\\n103\tInter-annotator $\\kappa$ & 0.67 \\\\\n104\tInter-annotator \\% agreement & 81\\% \\\\\n105\tGold stagnation ratio & 41.5\\% \\\\\n106\tLF stagnation ratio (same sample) & 33.5\\% \\\\\n107\tAdjudicated windows & 124 (19.1\\%) \\\\\n108\t\\bottomrule\n109\t\\end{tabular}\n110\t\\end{table}\n111\t\n112\tThe gold stagnation ratio (41.5\\%) significantly exceeds the LF ratio (33.5\\%), indicating that LFs systematically under-report stagnation---particularly cases where agents issue diverse commands but make no semantic progress.\n113\t\n114\t%==============================================================================\n115\t\\section{LF Label Validation Against Gold}\n116\t\\label{sec:lf-validation}\n117\t%==============================================================================\n118\t\n119\tTable~\\ref{tab:lf-gold} reports LF label accuracy against gold annotations, stratified by confidence.\n120\t\n121\t\\begin{table}[t]\n122\t\\centering\n123\t\\small\n124\t\\caption{LF label accuracy vs.\\ gold annotations by confidence tier.}\n125\t\\label{tab:lf-gold}\n126\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n127\t\\toprule\n128\tConfidence & $N$ & Accuracy & $\\kappa$ & Prec$_\\text{stag}$ & Rec$_\\text{stag}$ \\\\\n129\t\\midrule\n130\tconf=3 & 60 & 0.92 & 0.83 & 0.94 & 0.88 \\\\\n131\tconf=2 & 340 & 0.82 & 0.62 & 0.84 & 0.73 \\\\\n132\tconf=1 & 250 & 0.51 & 0.08 & 0.55 & 0.41 \\\\\n133\t\\midrule\n134\tOverall & 650 & 0.72 & 0.61 & 0.78 & 0.63 \\\\\n135\t\\bottomrule\n136\t\\end{tabular}\n137\t\\end{table}\n138\t\n139\tAt conf$\\geq$2, LF labels achieve 82\\% accuracy ($\\kappa\\!=\\!0.62$), validating their use as training signal for this tier. However, conf=1 windows (72\\% of the full dataset: 39,408 of 54,809) have near-random accuracy (0.51, $\\kappa\\!=\\!0.08$), explaining why models trained on all data underperform those trained on conf$\\geq$2 subsets.\n140\t\n141\tTable~\\ref{tab:lf-confusion} shows the overall confusion matrix. LF precision for stagnation is reasonably high (0.78)---when LFs flag stagnation, they are usually correct---but recall is low (0.63), systematically missing 37\\% of human-annotated stagnation. These missed cases constitute the \\emph{semantic residual} analyzed in \\S\\ref{sec:taxonomy}.\n142\t\n143\t\\begin{table}[t]\n144\t\\centering\n145\t\\small\n146\t\\caption{LF vs.\\ gold confusion matrix (overall, $N\\!=\\!650$).}\n147\t\\label{tab:lf-confusion}\n148\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n149\t\\toprule\n150\t & Gold=Stag & Gold=Prog \\\\\n151\t\\midrule\n152\tLF=Stag & 170 (TP) & 48 (FP) \\\\\n153\tLF=Prog & 100 (FN) & 332 (TN) \\\\\n154\t\\bottomrule\n155\t\\end{tabular}\n156\t\\end{table}\n157\t\n158\tThe dominant false-positive pattern is \\texttt{cmd\\_rep} firing on productive-but-repetitive debugging sequences. The dominant false-negative pattern is semantic stagnation with diverse commands---LFs see activity, humans see no progress.\n159\t\n160\t%==============================================================================\n161\t\\section{Structural Features Dominate Current Approaches}\n162\t\\label{sec:structural-eval}\n163\t%==============================================================================\n164\t\n165\t\\subsection{Models}\n166\t\n167\tWe compare three model families on the gold set:\n168\t\n169\t\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=1pt]\n170\t\\item \\textbf{Structural classifiers}: Gradient Boosted (GB), Random Forest (RF), and Logistic Regression (LR) over 30 lightweight features (command repetition ratio, unique command fraction, file diff counts, edit sizes, error frequencies, etc.). Inference time: $<$5ms per window.\n171\t\\item \\textbf{Neural probes}: GRU-based sequence classifiers operating on frozen embeddings from three encoders (Qwen3-0.6B, E5-large-v2, BGE-m3).\n172\t\\item \\textbf{LLM-as-judge}: Claude Sonnet, zero-shot prompting on window summaries.\n173\t\\end{itemize}\n174\t\n175\tAll models are trained on LF-derived labels (conf$\\geq$2 subset) and evaluated on the 650-window gold set.\n176\t\n177\t\\subsection{Gold Evaluation Results}\n178\t\n179\tTable~\\ref{tab:gold-eval} reports gold-set performance alongside the original LF-evaluated scores, revealing the magnitude of circular evaluation inflation.\n180\t\n181\t\\begin{table}[t]\n182\t\\centering\n183\t\\small\n184\t\\caption{Model performance on gold labels vs.\\ LF-derived labels, showing circular evaluation inflation ($\\Delta$).}\n185\t\\label{tab:gold-eval}\n186\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n187\t\\toprule\n188\tModel & LF-eval F1 & Gold-eval F1 & $\\Delta$ (pp) \\\\\n189\t\\midrule\n190\tStructural GB & 0.995 & 0.76 & $-$23.5 \\\\\n191\tStructural RF & 0.995 & 0.74 & $-$25.5 \\\\\n192\tStructural LR & 0.987 & 0.73 & $-$25.7 \\\\\n193\tProbe (GRU, Qwen3) & 0.906 & 0.69 & $-$21.6 \\\\\n194\tLLM-as-judge & 0.663 & 0.60 & $-$6.3 \\\\\n195\t\\bottomrule\n196\t\\end{tabular}\n197\t\\end{table}\n198\t\n199\t\\paragraph{Circular evaluation inflation.}\n200\tStructural classifiers drop $\\sim$22--26pp from LF-eval to gold-eval, while the LLM-as-judge drops only 6.3pp. This asymmetry is expected: structural features are direct inputs to the LFs that generated the training labels, so near-perfect LF-eval scores reflect reverse-engineering of the labeling rules rather than genuine detection capability. The LLM-as-judge, never fitted to LF labels, is least affected."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PuqCM1RDwcW4k2cPdFot2U","session_id":"0b19c43c-1b27-4951-bda8-40543516a891","uuid":"c3e31c3e-b526-4bdb-ae7b-20886e92e5db","timestamp":"2026-05-14T19:27:00.376Z"}
