{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BSiugfEmXmdtQ85RPHhV4t","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011T2jT7K98nbsbxutA9XYvS","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T184058/median/paper.tex","offset":200,"limit":300},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8520,"cache_read_input_tokens":60082,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8520,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PuqCM1RDwcW4k2cPdFot2U","session_id":"0b19c43c-1b27-4951-bda8-40543516a891","uuid":"e65ed331-8865-4faa-8747-57e4833d912d"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011T2jT7K98nbsbxutA9XYvS","type":"tool_result","content":"200\tStructural classifiers drop $\\sim$22--26pp from LF-eval to gold-eval, while the LLM-as-judge drops only 6.3pp. This asymmetry is expected: structural features are direct inputs to the LFs that generated the training labels, so near-perfect LF-eval scores reflect reverse-engineering of the labeling rules rather than genuine detection capability. The LLM-as-judge, never fitted to LF labels, is least affected.\n201\t\n202\t\\paragraph{Structural features still dominate.}\n203\tDespite the deflation, structural GB (0.76) remains the best performer on gold labels, outperforming the best neural probe (BGE-m3, 0.72) by 4pp and the LLM-as-judge (0.60) by 16pp. On the conf$\\geq$2 gold subset ($n\\!=\\!400$), structural GB reaches 0.82, demonstrating strong performance where LF labels are reliable. Even on conf=1 windows ($n\\!=\\!250$), structural GB (0.67) exceeds the LF accuracy (0.51) by 16pp, indicating that the model learns smoother decision boundaries beyond individual LF rules.\n204\t\n205\t\\subsection{Encoder Comparison for Neural Probes}\n206\t\n207\tTable~\\ref{tab:probe-variants} compares probe variants by encoder.\n208\t\n209\t\\begin{table}[t]\n210\t\\centering\n211\t\\small\n212\t\\caption{Probe variants on gold labels by encoder.}\n213\t\\label{tab:probe-variants}\n214\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n215\t\\toprule\n216\tEncoder & LF-eval F1 & Gold-eval F1 & $\\Delta$ vs.\\ Struct.\\ GB \\\\\n217\t\\midrule\n218\tQwen3-0.6B & 0.906 & 0.69 & $-$7pp \\\\\n219\tE5-large-v2 & 0.936 & 0.71 & $-$5pp \\\\\n220\tBGE-m3 & 0.941 & 0.72 & $-$4pp \\\\\n221\t\\bottomrule\n222\t\\end{tabular}\n223\t\\end{table}\n224\t\n225\tBGE-m3 emerges as the strongest probe encoder on gold labels (0.72), despite smaller margins on LF-eval. This ranking reversal suggests that encoder generalization differences are compressed by circular evaluation and only surface on independent gold labels. The structural--probe gap narrows from 8.96pp (LF-eval) to 7pp (gold), indicating that embeddings capture marginal semantic signal invisible to structural features---but not enough to close the gap.\n226\t\n227\t\\subsection{LLM-as-Judge Error Decomposition}\n228\t\n229\tTable~\\ref{tab:llm-errors} decomposes LLM-judge errors relative to LF labels on the gold set.\n230\t\n231\t\\begin{table}[t]\n232\t\\centering\n233\t\\small\n234\t\\caption{LLM-judge vs.\\ LF error decomposition on gold ($N\\!=\\!650$).}\n235\t\\label{tab:llm-errors}\n236\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n237\t\\toprule\n238\tCategory & Count & \\% \\\\\n239\t\\midrule\n240\tBoth correct & 398 & 61.2\\% \\\\\n241\tLLM correct, LF wrong & 53 & 8.2\\% \\\\\n242\tLLM wrong, LF correct & 94 & 14.5\\% \\\\\n243\tBoth wrong & 40 & 6.1\\% \\\\\n244\tAmbiguous (adjudicated) & 65 & 10.0\\% \\\\\n245\t\\bottomrule\n246\t\\end{tabular}\n247\t\\end{table}\n248\t\n249\tThe 8.2\\% of windows where the LLM is correct but LFs are wrong represent cases where semantic understanding adds genuine value---typically ``active but ineffective'' patterns where the agent edits files but the edits are logically flawed. However, the LLM errs more often than it helps (14.5\\% wrong vs.\\ 8.2\\% uniquely correct), often misclassifying verbose error output as stagnation. The net effect: the LLM-as-judge's independent semantic judgment is insufficiently reliable for monitoring, though it identifies real failure modes that structural approaches miss.\n250\t\n251\t%==============================================================================\n252\t\\section{LF Temporal Dynamics}\n253\t\\label{sec:temporal}\n254\t%==============================================================================\n255\t\n256\t\\subsection{Onset Alignment with Gold Annotations}\n257\t\n258\tTable~\\ref{tab:onset} reports alignment between LF first-fire and human-annotated stagnation onset across the 270 gold stagnation windows.\n259\t\n260\t\\begin{table}[t]\n261\t\\centering\n262\t\\small\n263\t\\caption{LF onset alignment with gold annotations ($N\\!=\\!270$ stagnation windows).}\n264\t\\label{tab:onset}\n265\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n266\t\\toprule\n267\tLF & Mean Offset (steps) & Within $\\pm$2 & Within $\\pm$3 \\\\\n268\t\\midrule\n269\t\\texttt{cmd\\_rep} & $-$1.2 & 48\\% & 61\\% \\\\\n270\t\\texttt{file\\_diff} & $-$0.6 & 56\\% & 68\\% \\\\\n271\t\\texttt{outcome\\_bp} & +5.3 & 22\\% & 34\\% \\\\\n272\tAny LF (first fire) & $-$0.8 & 52\\% & 64\\% \\\\\n273\t\\bottomrule\n274\t\\end{tabular}\n275\t\\end{table}\n276\t\n277\t\\texttt{file\\_diff} shows the best alignment with human-perceived onset (56\\% within $\\pm$2 steps, mean offset $-$0.6), consistent with ``no file changes'' being an intuitive stagnation signal shared by humans and LFs. \\texttt{cmd\\_rep} fires slightly early ($-$1.2 steps), detecting structural repetition before humans perceive stagnation. \\texttt{outcome\\_bp} lags significantly (+5.3 steps) by design, as it propagates outcome information backward.\n278\t\n279\tOverall onset alignment of 52\\% within $\\pm$2 steps confirms that LF temporal dynamics capture a real statistical trend, but with substantial noise ($\\pm$2--3 steps, $\\sigma\\!=\\!2.1$). These findings support Claim~2 with the caveat that onset timing is a coarse indicator rather than a precise intervention trigger.\n280\t\n281\t\\subsection{Agent-Specific Temporal Roles}\n282\t\n283\tOn SWE-Agent trajectories (from LF-eval data), \\texttt{cmd\\_rep} leads stagnation onset by $-$1.87 steps while on OpenHands it reverses to a lagging role (+3.32 steps). This role reversal reflects architectural differences: OpenHands' broader action repertoire reduces literal command repetition, making \\texttt{cmd\\_rep} fire late (92.3\\% firing rate, 71\\% false positive rate on OH). Ablation confirms that removing \\texttt{cmd\\_rep} from the OH labeling pipeline improves F1 by +21.6pp, demonstrating that LF behavior is not transferable across agent architectures without recalibration.\n284\t\n285\t%==============================================================================\n286\t\\section{Stagnation Type Taxonomy}\n287\t\\label{sec:taxonomy}\n288\t%==============================================================================\n289\t\n290\tError analysis on the gold set reveals a taxonomy of stagnation types (Table~\\ref{tab:taxonomy}).\n291\t\n292\t\\begin{table}[t]\n293\t\\centering\n294\t\\small\n295\t\\caption{Stagnation type taxonomy on gold set ($N\\!=\\!270$ stagnation windows).}\n296\t\\label{tab:taxonomy}\n297\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n298\t\\toprule\n299\tType & $N$ (\\%) & Struct.\\ F1 & Probe F1 & LLM F1 \\\\\n300\t\\midrule\n301\tRepetitive-structural & 146 (54.1\\%) & 0.88 & 0.79 & 0.58 \\\\\n302\tLow-activity stagnation & 46 (17.0\\%) & 0.83 & 0.72 & 0.55 \\\\\n303\t\\midrule\n304\tStrategic dead-end & 31 (11.5\\%) & 0.22 & 0.28 & 0.38 \\\\\n305\tImplicit repetition & 24 (8.9\\%) & 0.31 & 0.35 & 0.33 \\\\\n306\tUnfocused exploration & 23 (8.5\\%) & 0.18 & 0.24 & 0.30 \\\\\n307\t\\bottomrule\n308\t\\end{tabular}\n309\t\\end{table}\n310\t\n311\t\\paragraph{Structural stagnation (71.1\\%).}\n312\tThe majority of gold stagnation is \\emph{structural}: repetitive command patterns (54.1\\%) and low file activity (17.0\\%). All methods detect these effectively, with structural classifiers performing best (F1\\,=\\,0.83--0.88).\n313\t\n314\t\\paragraph{Semantic residual (28.9\\%).}\n315\tThe remaining 78 windows (28.9\\% of gold stagnation) constitute the \\emph{semantic residual}---stagnation that no current automated method captures effectively:\n316\t\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=1pt]\n317\t\\item \\textbf{Strategic dead-ends} (11.5\\%): The agent pursues a coherent but fundamentally wrong approach. Commands are diverse, files change, no LF fires---only understanding the task goal reveals the futility.\n318\t\\item \\textbf{Implicit repetition} (8.9\\%): The agent retries the same logical approach with different surface commands (e.g., \\texttt{grep} $\\to$ \\texttt{find} $\\to$ \\texttt{ag}). Literal command repetition is low, but intent-level repetition is high.\n319\t\\item \\textbf{Unfocused exploration} (8.5\\%): Rapid switching between non-viable approaches. Structurally resembles productive exploration; semantically is directionless thrashing.\n320\t\\end{itemize}\n321\t\n322\tAll methods achieve F1$\\,\\leq\\,$0.38 on semantic residual types. The LLM-as-judge performs best on strategic dead-ends (0.38), leveraging partial intent understanding, but still fails far more than it succeeds. Probes and structural classifiers are near-blind to these categories. This 28.9\\% semantic residual represents the structural ceiling of current approaches and a concrete target for future task-aware methods.\n323\t\n324\t%==============================================================================\n325\t\\section{Fine-Grained Performance Analysis}\n326\t\\label{sec:breakdown}\n327\t%==============================================================================\n328\t\n329\tTable~\\ref{tab:breakdown} reports stagnation F1 broken down by trajectory position, dominant LF, and outcome on the LF test set ($N\\!=\\!1{,}824$, conf$\\geq$2).\n330\t\n331\t\\begin{table}[t]\n332\t\\centering\n333\t\\small\n334\t\\caption{F1$_\\text{stag}$ breakdown by dimension (LF test set, conf$\\geq$2).}\n335\t\\label{tab:breakdown}\n336\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n337\t\\toprule\n338\tDimension & Subcategory & $N_\\text{stag}$ & F1$_\\text{stag}$ & $\\Delta$ vs.\\ overall \\\\\n339\t\\midrule\n340\t\\multirow{3}{*}{Position} & Early (0--33\\%) & 98 & 0.88 & +2.1pp \\\\\n341\t & Middle (33--66\\%) & 132 & 0.79 & $-$6.9pp \\\\\n342\t & Late (66--100\\%) & 255 & 0.87 & +1.1pp \\\\\n343\t\\midrule\n344\t\\multirow{4}{*}{Dom.\\ LF} & \\texttt{cmd\\_rep} & 291 & 0.90 & +4.1pp \\\\\n345\t & \\texttt{file\\_diff} & 108 & 0.83 & $-$2.9pp \\\\\n346\t & \\texttt{outcome\\_bp} & 42 & 0.72 & $-$13.9pp \\\\\n347\t & multi-signal & 44 & 0.94 & +8.1pp \\\\\n348\t\\midrule\n349\t\\multirow{2}{*}{Outcome} & Resolved & 8 & 0.94 & +8.1pp \\\\\n350\t & Unresolved & 477 & 0.85 & $-$0.9pp \\\\\n351\t\\bottomrule\n352\t\\end{tabular}\n353\t\\end{table}\n354\t\n355\tThree patterns emerge. First, \\textbf{mid-trajectory stagnation is hardest} (F1\\,=\\,0.79), reflecting ambiguous transitional states where stagnation onset/offset boundaries are gradual rather than crisp. Second, \\textbf{\\texttt{outcome\\_bp}-dominated windows are the weakest} (F1\\,=\\,0.72) because this lagging signal fires well after stagnation begins, creating label boundary noise. Third, \\textbf{multi-signal agreement yields the cleanest labels} (F1\\,=\\,0.94), confirming that LF consensus is a reliable quality indicator.\n356\t\n357\tOn the gold set, the structural--semantic split is stark: structural stagnation achieves probe F1\\,=\\,0.78, while semantic stagnation drops to 0.27---a 51pp gap that underscores the fundamental limitation of training on LF-derived labels for detecting non-structural failure modes.\n358\t\n359\t%==============================================================================\n360\t\\section{Temporal Label Models: A Well-Motivated Negative Finding}\n361\t\\label{sec:tlm}\n362\t%==============================================================================\n363\t\n364\tThe systematic temporal dynamics of LFs (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal}) motivate temporal label models (TLMs) that explicitly model LF firing sequences rather than treating each window independently.\n365\t\n366\t\\subsection{Implementations}\n367\t\n368\t\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=1pt]\n369\t\\item \\textbf{TWV (Temporally Weighted Voting)}: Gaussian-weighted votes where recent LF firings receive higher weight. Controlled by bandwidth $\\sigma$.\n370\t\\item \\textbf{HMM-TLM}: Hidden Markov Model with latent stagnation/progress states and LF emissions, modeling transition dynamics.\n371\t\\item \\textbf{Lag-aware HMM}: HMM variant incorporating learned per-LF lag offsets to align temporally shifted signals.\n372\t\\end{itemize}\n373\t\n374\t\\subsection{Results}\n375\t\n376\tTWV is harmful for the vast majority of $\\sigma$ values. The only weakly positive configuration ($\\sigma\\!=\\!2$) produces labels that, when used to train downstream structural classifiers, degrade performance across all evaluation settings: in-domain $-$4.9pp, cross-agent $-$21.7pp, conf$\\geq$2 $-$1.8pp. HMM-TLM overpredicts the stagnation class. Lag-aware HMM matches but does not exceed majority vote (F1\\,=\\,0.894 vs.\\ MV 0.894).\n377\t\n378\t\\subsection{Failure Analysis}\n379\t\n380\tThe failure of all three TLM variants traces to a common root cause: the low-conflict regime of the current 3-LF framework. At conf$\\geq$2, LFs agree 95.1\\% of the time, leaving minimal room for temporal re-weighting to improve. TWV's primary effect is changing labels in the low-confidence (conf=1) stratum, but it changes them in the wrong direction---introducing noise rather than correcting errors. This negative finding provides an important constraint: temporal label models require higher LF diversity and disagreement rates to offer benefits over simple majority vote.\n381\t\n382\t%==============================================================================\n383\t\\section{Discussion}\n384\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n385\t%==============================================================================\n386\t\n387\t\\paragraph{Reinterpreting ``structural dominance.''}\n388\tOur gold-validated results support a nuanced conclusion: LF-approximated stagnation is structural in nature, and structural features remain the best practical choice for detection given their efficiency ($<$5ms) and cross-domain robustness. However, human-recognizable stagnation has additional semantic dimensions that current NL methods---including neural probes and LLM-as-judge---have not yet effectively captured. We do not claim that semantic understanding is valueless, but rather that current automated approaches to encoding semantics fail to capture the task-level reasoning required to detect strategic dead-ends, implicit repetition, and unfocused exploration.\n389\t\n390\t\\paragraph{Circular evaluation as a methodological warning.}\n391\tThe $\\sim$22pp inflation from LF-eval to gold-eval is a cautionary finding for any weak supervision study. When structural features serve as inputs to both the labeling functions and the downstream classifier, near-perfect LF-eval scores reflect rule reconstruction, not genuine capability. We recommend that studies using programmatic labeling always report the circular evaluation gap alongside headline numbers.\n392\t\n393\t\\paragraph{Practical implications.}\n394\tFor deployment, structural classifiers offer the best cost--performance tradeoff: lightweight, fast, and outperforming alternatives on both LF-derived and human-validated labels. The 28.9\\% semantic residual represents a known blind spot that should be disclosed when deploying such monitors. For settings where this residual is critical, ensemble approaches combining structural features with targeted LLM evaluation of low-confidence windows may offer a practical compromise.\n395\t\n396\t%==============================================================================\n397\t\\section{Limitations}\n398\t\\label{sec:limitations}\n399\t%==============================================================================\n400\t\n401\t\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=1pt]\n402\t\\item \\textbf{Domain scope.} All data comes from software engineering agents (SWE-bench). While structural stagnation patterns (command repetition, inactivity) likely generalize, the specific LF thresholds and taxonomy categories may not transfer to non-SE domains.\n403\t\\item \\textbf{Agent scope.} Cross-agent evaluation covers only two agents (SWE-Agent, OpenHands). The \\texttt{cmd\\_rep} role reversal on OpenHands demonstrates that findings are agent-specific; broader agent coverage is needed.\n404\t\\item \\textbf{Gold set size.} The 650-window gold set, while sufficient for the comparisons reported, limits statistical power for fine-grained subgroup analyses (e.g., $n\\!=\\!23$ for unfocused exploration).\n405\t\\item \\textbf{LLM-as-judge.} We evaluate a single LLM (Claude Sonnet) in a zero-shot setting. Few-shot or fine-tuned LLM judges may perform better, though at higher cost.\n406\t\\item \\textbf{Temporal noise.} Onset alignment ($\\pm$2--3 steps) reflects both genuine measurement noise and the inherent subjectivity of stagnation onset boundaries.\n407\t\\end{itemize}\n408\t\n409\t%==============================================================================\n410\t\\section{Conclusion}\n411\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n412\t%==============================================================================\n413\t\n414\tWe present a systematic empirical study of progress stagnation detection in LLM agents, validated against 650 human-annotated windows. Our findings deliver strong empirical evidence with identified limitations: structural features dominate all current automated approaches (gold macro-F1\\,=\\,0.76 vs.\\ 0.72 for the best probe and 0.60 for LLM-as-judge), but 28.9\\% of human-recognized stagnation constitutes a semantic residual beyond their reach. LF temporal dynamics provide useful coarse-grained onset signals ($\\pm$2--3 step resolution), and temporal label models fail to improve over majority vote in the current low-conflict LF regime.\n415\t\n416\tThese results redirect agent monitoring research along two axes: toward lightweight structural approaches for practical deployment, and toward task-aware semantic methods for the identified residual. The 28.9\\% semantic ceiling---comprising strategic dead-ends, implicit repetition, and unfocused exploration---provides a concrete benchmark for future progress.\n417\t\n418\t%==============================================================================\n419\t% References (placeholder for review-readiness)\n420\t%==============================================================================\n421\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n422\t\n423\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n424\t\n425\t\\bibitem[Yang et al.(2024)]{sweagent}\n426\tJ.~Yang, C.~E.~Jimenez, A.~Wettig, K.~Lieret, S.~Xia, K.~Narasimhan, and O.~Press.\n427\t\\newblock {SWE-agent}: Agent-computer interfaces enable automated software engineering.\n428\t\\newblock \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2024.\n429\t\n430\t\\bibitem[Wang et al.(2024)]{openhands}\n431\tX.~Wang, B.~Li, Y.~Song, et~al.\n432\t\\newblock {OpenHands}: An open platform for {AI} software developers as generalist agents.\n433\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.16741}, 2024.\n434\t\n435\t\\bibitem[Ratner et al.(2017)]{snorkel}\n436\tA.~Ratner, S.~H.~Bach, H.~Ehrenberg, J.~Fries, S.~Wu, and C.~R\\'{e}.\n437\t\\newblock Snorkel: Rapid training data creation with weak supervision.\n438\t\\newblock \\emph{VLDB}, 2017.\n439\t\n440\t\\bibitem[Xie et al.(2026)]{agentprm}\n441\tZ.~Xie et~al.\n442\t\\newblock {AgentPRM}: Process reward model for agent training via {TD} and {GAE}.\n443\t\\newblock \\emph{ACM Web Conference}, 2026.\n444\t\n445\t\\bibitem[Shinn et al.(2023)]{reflexion}\n446\tN.~Shinn, F.~Cassano, A.~Gopinath, K.~Narasimhan, and S.~Yao.\n447\t\\newblock Reflexion: Language agents with verbal reinforcement learning.\n448\t\\newblock \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n449\t\n450\t\\bibitem[Zhou et al.(2023)]{lats}\n451\tA.~Zhou, Y.~Yan, M.~Shlapentokh-Rothman, H.~Wang, and Y.-X.~Wang.\n452\t\\newblock Language agent tree search unifies reasoning acting and planning in language models.\n453\t\\newblock \\emph{ICML}, 2024.\n454\t\n455\t\\bibitem[Yao et al.(2023)]{tot}\n456\tS.~Yao, D.~Yu, J.~Zhao, et~al.\n457\t\\newblock Tree of thoughts: Deliberate problem solving with large language models.\n458\t\\newblock \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n459\t\n460\t\\bibitem[Jiang et al.(2026)]{slopcodebench}\n461\tY.~Jiang et~al.\n462\t\\newblock {SlopCodeBench}: Benchmarking coding agent degradation in extended contexts.\n463\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.24755}, 2026.\n464\t\n465\t\\bibitem[Jimenez et al.(2024)]{swebench}\n466\tC.~E.~Jimenez, J.~Yang, A.~Wettig, et~al.\n467\t\\newblock {SWE-bench}: Can language models resolve real-world {GitHub} issues?\n468\t\\newblock \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n469\t\n470\t\\bibitem[Chen et al.(2025)]{monitoring}\n471\tX.~Chen et~al.\n472\t\\newblock Monitoring {LLM} agents for sequentially contextual harm.\n473\t\\newblock \\emph{ICLR Workshop}, 2025.\n474\t\n475\t\\end{thebibliography}\n476\t\n477\t\\end{document}\n478\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PuqCM1RDwcW4k2cPdFot2U","session_id":"0b19c43c-1b27-4951-bda8-40543516a891","uuid":"3d7c8d9b-bba3-4f0d-bf54-c70a3ac6ee4d","timestamp":"2026-05-14T19:27:05.409Z"}
