{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EsUZyxqeCh4dbNJ6dhySzb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260514T184058\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: Weak Reject (3/6)\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-14T19:17:51.067941+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report\n9\t\n10\t**Paper:** Stagnation Signals Are Structural, Not Semantic: A Systematic Empirical Study of Progress Detection in LLM Agents\n11\t\n12\t**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers, 4-step roundtable)\n13\t\n14\t**Overall Score: Weak Reject (3/6)**\n15\t\n16\t---\n17\t\n18\t## Consolidated Summary\n19\t\n20\tThis paper presents an empirical study comparing structural features, neural embedding probes, and LLM-as-judge for detecting progress stagnation in LLM agents. Using a weak supervision pipeline with 3 labeling functions and 650 gold-annotated windows from SWE-Agent trajectories, it demonstrates that gradient boosting over 30 hand-crafted structural features achieves 0.89 gold macro-F1, surpassing GRU probes (0.81) and Claude Sonnet zero-shot (0.67). The paper quantifies the circular evaluation artifact (~10.5pp from in-domain to gold) and reports a well-motivated negative result on temporal label models.\n21\t\n22\tThe paper is competent and honest, but falls below the bar for a top ML venue (~25% acceptance) due to limited methodological novelty, narrow evaluation scope (2 SE agents), missing statistical significance tests, and an unfairly weak LLM-judge baseline.\n23\t\n24\t---\n25\t\n26\t## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n27\t\n28\t### Strengths\n29\t\n30\t- **S1: Circularity decomposition is the paper's strongest contribution.** The explicit quantification of in-domain 0.995 → gold 0.89 (~10.5pp gap) is rigorous and honest. The observation that the LLM-judge is the only method whose gold performance does not decline (+0.8pp) provides an elegant internal consistency check confirming that LF-correlated methods show circularity drops while LF-orthogonal methods do not.\n31\t\n32\t- **S2: Feature importance stability analysis (Spearman ρ≈0.95)** between LF-derived and gold evaluation demonstrates that circularity inflates magnitude but does not alter directional signal, strengthening the claim that structural features capture genuine stagnation patterns.\n33\t\n34\t- **S3: Gold annotation protocol is adequate.** 650 windows, 2 annotators, κ=0.81, disagreements resolved by discussion. The stratified analysis by LF confidence (conf=1: 0.68, conf≥2: 0.93, conf=3: 0.98) reveals a clean monotonic relationship validating the LF pipeline design.\n35\t\n36\t- **S4: The three-way method comparison on gold labels is the right experimental design** for an empirical analysis paper. Each method class represents a natural point on the cost-complexity spectrum.\n37\t\n38\t- **S5: The TLM negative result is well-grounded.** Three implementations all fail, with TWV degrading downstream by 21.7pp. The mechanistic explanation (low-conflict regime, 95.1% conf≥2 agreement) is satisfying.\n39\t\n40\t### Weaknesses\n41\t\n42\t- **W1: Gold set covers SWE-Agent only; cross-agent claims lack independent validation.** The cross-agent 0.967 F1 on OpenHands is evaluated entirely on LF-derived labels. The paper applies the SWE-Agent circularity gap to bound OpenHands gold performance at [0.83, 0.92], but this extrapolation assumes the circularity structure transfers across agents — unjustified given that LF failure modes differ qualitatively between agents (e.g., cmd_rep has 71% false positive rate on OpenHands).\n43\t\n44\t- **W2: No confidence intervals or significance tests on any gold-set comparison.** The structural-vs-probe gap on gold is 5–8pp with N=650. Whether the 5pp structural-vs-BGE gap is statistically significant is left unaddressed. This is a serious omission for a top-venue empirical paper.\n45\t\n46\t- **W3: Subtype analysis (Table 9) and trajectory position analysis (Table 10) may mix LF-derived and gold labels.** Table 9 reports 485 stagnation windows, but the gold set contains only ~214 stagnation windows. The evaluation regime is not clarified, creating ambiguity about circularity.\n47\t\n48\t- **W4: The 30 structural features are not fully specified.** Only 5 are named; the remaining 25 are undocumented. For the paper's primary recommended approach, complete specification is essential for reproducibility.\n49\t\n50\t- **W5: MV LF labels (0.92 gold macro-F1) outperform the trained GB classifier (0.89).** This means the raw LF majority vote, without any learning, beats the structural classifier by 3pp. The paper includes this row with an asterisk but does not address the implication: if MV already achieves 0.92, what additional value does training GB provide?\n51\t\n52\t- **W6: LLM-judge baseline limited to zero-shot Claude Sonnet.** No few-shot, no chain-of-thought, no other LLMs tested. The 0.67 may reflect weak prompting rather than a fundamental limitation.\n53\t\n54\t- **W7: Temporal onset alignment uses ±2 step tolerance on k=5 windows (40% margin).** Alignment at stricter tolerances (±1, exact match) is not reported. The 80% alignment may partly reflect tolerance generosity.\n55\t\n56\t### Questions for Authors\n57\t\n58\t- Q1: Does the MV LF label superiority (0.92 vs 0.89) mean the GB classifier is strictly worse than simply applying LFs at test time?\n59\t- Q2: How many of the 85 gold onset boundaries come from trajectories overlapping with the structural classifier's training set?\n60\t- Q3: Can you provide direct analysis of cases where the probe is correct and structural features are wrong on gold?\n61\t\n62\t### Scores\n63\t- Soundness: 3/4\n64\t- Empirical Rigor: 3/4\n65\t\n66\t---\n67\t\n68\t## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n69\t\n70\t### Strengths\n71\t\n72\t- **S1: The structural dominance finding is empirically valuable despite being intuitive.** Showing that command repetition ratios outperform embeddings by 8–22pp on gold is a practically useful result that could redirect engineering effort. The magnitude of the gap is the contribution.\n73\t\n74\t- **S2: The circularity decomposition is a genuine methodological contribution** providing a reusable framework for weak supervision studies.\n75\t\n76\t- **S3: The temporal dynamics analysis reveals a non-obvious cross-agent finding.** The cmd_rep role reversal between SWE-Agent (leading, −1.87 steps) and OpenHands (lagging, +3.32 steps), with +21.6pp improvement from removing cmd_rep on OpenHands, is concrete and actionable.\n77\t\n78\t- **S4: Honest treatment of the negative TLM result** with a plausible mechanistic explanation.\n79\t\n80\t### Weaknesses\n81\t\n82\t- **W1: Limited methodological novelty.** The paper employs entirely standard components (majority vote, gradient boosting, GRU probes, zero-shot LLM). There is no new algorithm, model, or inference procedure. The novelty rests entirely on the finding, which has a \"confirm the obvious\" quality: structural LFs naturally produce labels that structural features predict. The gold-validated residual (0.89) is the genuine contribution, but methodological novelty is near zero.\n83\t\n84\t- **W2: Narrow evaluation scope severely limits significance claims.** Both agents are SE agents on SWE-bench. The title and abstract make sweeping claims about \"LLM Agents\" without qualification. Structural features are heavily SE-specific (file diff count, edit diversity). Whether these transfer to web navigation, scientific discovery, or conversational agents is entirely unknown.\n85\t\n86\t- **W3: The \"semantic stagnation\" framing is undermined by the paper's own results.** The paper motivates itself with detecting \"semantically diverse but collectively ineffective\" behavior. Yet Table 9 shows the \"outcome-only\" subtype (true semantic stagnation) has the lowest detection (F1_stag=0.72), confirming structural features struggle with the very phenomenon the paper claims to address. This tension deserves direct confrontation.\n87\t\n88\t- **W4: Gold set is SWE-Agent only** (same as R1-W1). Cross-agent claims rest on circular evaluation.\n89\t\n90\t- **W5: The LLM-judge baseline is unfairly weak** (same as R1-W6). A single zero-shot prompt achieving 0.67 is unsurprising and does not constitute a fair comparison.\n91\t\n92\t- **W6: No statistical significance tests** (same as R1-W2).\n93\t\n94\t- **W7: Temporal onset validation rests on N=85 onset events** — a small sample for per-LF breakdown with ±2 tolerance.\n95\t\n96\t### Missing Related Work\n97\t\n98\t- **George et al. 2025, \"Unsupervised Cycle Detection in Agentic Applications\" (arXiv 2511.10650):** Most directly comparable prior work on cycle/stagnation detection, combining call-stack structural analysis with embedding similarity. Significant omission.\n99\t- **Pipis et al. 2025, \"Wait, Wait, Wait... Why Do Reasoning Models Loop?\" (arXiv 2512.12895):** Analyzes looping in reasoning models using 30-gram repetition. Relevant to cmd_rep LF design.\n100\t- **Cemri et al. 2025, \"Why Do Multi-Agent LLM Systems Fail?\" (arXiv 2503.13657):** MAST taxonomy includes \"Step Repetition\" failure mode.\n101\t- **Safranchik et al. AAAI 2020, \"Weakly Supervised Sequence Tagging from Noisy Rules\":** Linked HMM for sequential weak supervision — closest methodological precedent for the TLM attempt. Essential for contextualizing the negative result.\n102\t- **Sala et al. NeurIPS 2019, \"Multi-Resolution Weak Supervision for Sequential Data\":** Direct precedent for weak supervision on sequential data with temporal structure.\n103\t- **Ratner et al. 2018 (Snorkel, VLDB)** and **FlyingSquid (Fu et al., ICML 2020):** Core weak supervision references surprisingly absent.\n104\t- **Dawid & Skene 1979** and broader label aggregation literature: The paper frames majority vote as the only i.i.d. alternative without discussing more sophisticated aggregation.\n105\t\n106\t### Questions for Authors\n107\t\n108\t- Q1: What performance does the LLM-judge achieve with few-shot prompting and chain-of-thought?\n109\t- Q2: How many of the 30 features are SE-specific vs. domain-general? Could you report a \"domain-general subset\" ablation?\n110\t- Q3: What does the probe achieve on the \"outcome-only\" stagnation subtype? If the probe outperforms structural there, the structural/semantic dichotomy requires nuancing.\n111\t- Q4: How close is structural GB (0.89) to the inter-annotator ceiling (~0.91 agreement)?\n112\t\n113\t### Scores\n114\t- Novelty: 2/4\n115\t- Significance: 2/4\n116\t\n117\t---\n118\t\n119\t## Roundtable Convergence\n120\t\n121\t### Points of Agreement (Both Reviewers)\n122\t\n123\t1. **No statistical significance tests** — the most critical gap. Both reviewers flag this as a serious omission for an empirical paper at a top venue with N=650.\n124\t2. **Gold set limited to SWE-Agent** — cross-agent claims are unvalidated on gold and the circularity gap extrapolation is methodologically unsound.\n125\t3. **Weak LLM-judge baseline** — zero-shot only, no few-shot/CoT variants tested. The -22pp gap may be an artifact of minimal prompting effort.\n126\t4. **Circularity decomposition is the strongest contribution** — both reviewers agree this is rigorous, honest, and potentially generalizable.\n127\t5. **TLM negative result is well-motivated** — honest reporting with mechanistic explanation.\n128\t6. **Temporal onset tolerance (±2 on k=5) is generous** — alignment at stricter tolerances needed.\n129\t\n130\t### Points of Divergence\n131\t\n132\t- **R1 leans toward borderline weak accept** (emphasizing empirical contribution value and circularity decomposition); **R2 leans toward weak reject** (emphasizing limited novelty and narrow scope).\n133\t- **R2 raises the framing tension** — the paper's \"semantic stagnation\" motivation is undermined by its own results showing structural methods fail on outcome-only stagnation (F1=0.72). R1 does not flag this.\n134\t- **R2 identifies significant missing related work** (6+ papers) that R1 does not discuss.\n135\t- **R1 uniquely catches the MV > GB tension** (0.92 vs 0.89 on gold) — a potentially damaging finding for the paper's practical message.\n136\t\n137\t### Consensus Score: Weak Reject (3/6)\n138\t\n139\tThe combination of limited methodological novelty, narrow evaluation scope (2 SE agents only), absent statistical significance tests, unfairly weak LLM-judge baseline, and several significant missing related works places this paper below the acceptance threshold for a top venue. The circularity decomposition framework and the well-motivated TLM negative result are genuine contributions, but insufficient to overcome the accumulated weaknesses.\n140\t\n141\t---\n142\t\n143\t## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n144\t\n145\t| Planned Experiment | Status | Notes |\n146\t|---|---|---|\n147\t| Gold-LF Agreement (κ) | ✅ Covered | Table 1, κ=0.84 |\n148\t| Gold-LF Conf≥2 Accuracy | ✅ Covered | Table 2, 0.93 accuracy |\n149\t| Structural on Gold | ✅ Covered | Table 3, GB 0.89 |\n150\t| Probe on Gold | ✅ Covered | Table 5, Qwen3 0.81 |\n151\t| LLM-Judge on Gold | ✅ Covered | Table 6, 0.67 |\n152\t| Temporal Onset Alignment | ✅ Covered | Table 7, 80% alignment |\n153\t| Cross-Agent Gold Validation | ⚠️ Partial | Discussion only, no gold annotation for OpenHands |\n154\t| Circularity Quantification | ✅ Covered | Table 8 |\n155\t| F1_stag by Confidence | ⚠️ Partial | Mentioned in text, no dedicated table |\n156\t| F1_stag by Position | ✅ Covered | Table 10 |\n157\t| F1_stag by Subtype | ✅ Covered | Table 9 |\n158\t| Probe vs Structural Error Decomp | ✅ Covered | Table 11 |\n159\t\n160\tCoverage: 9/12 fully covered, 2 partial, 1 missing (OpenHands gold). The paper reflects ~80% of the planned experiment matrix.\n161\t\n162\t---\n163\t\n164\t## Actionable Recommendations (Priority Order)\n165\t\n166\t1. **Add bootstrap 95% CIs and McNemar's test** to all gold-set method comparisons (structural vs probe, structural vs LLM-judge).\n167\t2. **Test LLM-judge with 5-shot prompting and CoT** to establish whether 0.67 is robust to prompting strategy.\n168\t3. **Hedge cross-agent claims explicitly** — state that OpenHands results are LF-derived only, with bounded estimates, not validated findings.\n169\t4. **Address the MV > GB tension** — explain why training a classifier is valuable when raw MV achieves higher gold F1.\n170\t5. **Add missing related work** — George et al. 2025, Safranchik et al. 2020, Snorkel, FlyingSquid at minimum.\n171\t6. **Confront the framing tension** — the \"semantic stagnation\" motivation vs. the structural dominance finding. Either reframe the motivation or discuss outcome-only stagnation (F1=0.72) as a limitation of the structural approach.\n172\t7. **Document all 30 features** in an appendix for reproducibility.\n173\t8. **Report temporal onset alignment at ±0 and ±1 tolerances** alongside ±2.\n174\t9. **Clarify evaluation regime** for Tables 9 and 10 (gold vs. LF-derived labels).\n175\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PuqCM1RDwcW4k2cPdFot2U","session_id":"0b19c43c-1b27-4951-bda8-40543516a891","uuid":"ac7af4f4-53f6-4f57-854a-107054c071cd","timestamp":"2026-05-14T19:27:13.739Z"}
