{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YCtSqhgHHVtRnbSQqtrnQK","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nBased on my thorough analysis of the project materials — the paper summary, experimental results, decision log, prior dream reviews, writing plan, and paper framing — here is my review.\n\n## R1 Review: Methodology and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper identifies a \"circular evaluation trap\" in weak supervision for agent stagnation detection, demonstrating that structural features achieve near-perfect F1 (0.995) on LF-derived labels but collapse to 0.55–0.60 on a 650-window human-annotated gold set. A 5-category failure taxonomy and negative results on three temporal label model implementations (TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM) are presented. The core message — that high scores on LF-derived labels measure fidelity to heuristic rules rather than real stagnation detection — is a genuine methodological insight, but the paper suffers from serious statistical gaps, an insufficiently validated gold set, and incomplete experimental analysis.\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: **The circular evaluation trap is the paper's strongest and most transferable contribution.** The mechanistic explanation is clear: structural features are continuous relaxations of LF inputs, so training on LF-derived labels and evaluating on LF-derived labels produces a tautological feedback loop. The 37–44pp collapse from LF-eval to gold-eval (Table 4) is a striking demonstration. This finding generalizes to any weak supervision pipeline where features correlate with the label-generating heuristics.\n- S2: **Comprehensive multi-method comparison on a shared gold set.** Evaluating structural classifiers (GB/RF/LR), three neural probe encoders (Qwen3/E5/BGE), and an LLM judge all on the same 650-window gold set with consistent metrics (Table 6) is methodologically clean and provides an apples-to-apples comparison that the field needs.\n- S3: **The failure taxonomy (Table 8) is descriptively valuable.** Decomposing LF failures into 5 categories with per-category LF recall (ranging from 0.27 for semantic-diverse stagnation to 0.91 for clear structural loops) reveals where the weak supervision pipeline breaks down. The finding that LFs only handle ~20% of gold stagnation well (the \"clear structural loops\" category) is a compelling indictment of naive LF-based approaches.\n- S4: **Honest and well-motivated negative results on TLMs.** Three distinct implementations (TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM) all fail to beat majority vote on gold labels (Table 11). The observation \"temporal alignment of noise is still noise\" is pithy and well-supported by the data. The failure analysis pointing to the low-conflict 3-LF regime is a useful constraint for future research.\n- S5: **The convergence finding (Table 6) is noteworthy.** All methods — structural, neural, LLM judge — converge to 0.55–0.60 F1 on gold, suggesting a shared ceiling imposed by the difficulty of the task and/or limitations in all current feature representations. This is a genuinely surprising finding that deserves more prominence.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: **No confidence intervals, significance tests, or bootstrap analyses anywhere in the paper.** This is the most critical gap. The core claim rests on differences of 1–5pp across methods on a 650-sample gold set (e.g., GB 0.58 vs. BGE 0.60 vs. LLM judge 0.56 in Table 6). Without McNemar's test or bootstrap 95% CIs, it is impossible to determine whether these differences are statistically meaningful or noise. For an empirical analysis paper at a top venue, this is a disqualifying omission. Furthermore, the gold set contains unbalanced subgroup sizes in the failure taxonomy (n=35 to n=82 per category), and the per-subtype F1 numbers in Table 9 (e.g., structural 0.25 vs. LLM 0.38 on semantic-diverse stagnation) are reported without any uncertainty quantification on samples this small.\n- W2: **No inter-annotator agreement (IAA) is reported for the gold set.** The paper acknowledges this in the Limitations section but does not report Cohen's kappa, percent agreement, or any IAA metric. For a 650-window gold set that serves as the foundation for every claim in the paper, this is a critical methodological hole. Without IAA, we cannot distinguish annotation noise from genuine model failures. If IAA kappa is, say, 0.65, then the gold F1 ceiling may itself be an artifact of annotation disagreement rather than a property of the methods under evaluation. The decision log reveals that earlier phases of the project struggled with label quality (D004: raw agreement only 53%, D006: 81.5% after excluding uncertain cases), raising legitimate concerns about whether the final gold annotations are reliable.\n- W3: **Gold set construction and sampling strategy are underspecified.** How were the 650 windows sampled? Were they stratified by confidence level, trajectory length, agent type, or LF agreement pattern? The distribution across the 5 failure categories (Table 8) suggests some stratification occurred (otherwise semantic-diverse stagnation at n=82 seems overrepresented relative to the base rate), but this is not documented. Selection bias in the gold set directly affects all downstream conclusions. Were any of these windows used during LF development or model training? The paper does not address potential contamination.\n- W4: **The retraining-on-gold experiment (GB retrained: 0.63 F1) is insufficiently analyzed.** This is presented as evidence that \"structural features lack information capacity for semantic stagnation,\" but with only 650 training samples, the poor performance could simply reflect insufficient data rather than inherent feature limitations. A learning curve (training on 100/200/400/650 gold samples) or cross-validated estimate would be needed to disentangle the two explanations. The project's own data-scaling experiments on LF labels showed monotonic improvement from 10% to 100% data (data-scaling-v2), so the 650-sample regime is far below where structural models saturate.\n- W5: **LLM judge baseline is unfairly weak.** Only zero-shot classification is tested (Table 6, F1=0.56). No few-shot prompting, no chain-of-thought, no instruction-tuned variants, no other LLMs. The claim that structural features \"decisively surpass\" LLM judges rests on a single zero-shot prompt. Given that few-shot prompting routinely improves LLM classification by 10–20pp, the 2pp gap between LLM judge (0.56) and structural GB (0.58) on gold could easily reverse with a fairer LLM baseline. This undermines one of the paper's headline claims.\n- W6: **Cross-agent evaluation (Table 7) lacks gold validation.** The OpenHands cross-agent results (SWE in-domain 0.995→0.58 on gold, OH cross-agent 0.967→0.52 on gold) appear to use gold labels for the SWE side but LF-derived labels for the OH side (the OH number 0.52 is described as gold, but the project's own records show no OH gold annotation was completed). If the OH \"gold\" evaluation actually uses LF-derived labels corrected by the same circularity the paper critiques, this is a serious methodological inconsistency. If genuine OH gold labels exist, the annotation protocol should be documented with the same rigor as the SWE gold set.\n- W7: **Temporal onset alignment (Table 10) lacks a null baseline.** 28% of onsets align within ±1 step — but what is the expected alignment under random firing? With k=5 windows and systematic class imbalance, a random null could produce non-trivial alignment by chance. Without this calibration, the 28% figure is uninterpretable. Additionally, the mean offset of 3.8 steps on k=5 windows represents a 76% window-width misalignment, which seems to undermine the claim that LF temporal dynamics are \"systematic.\"\n- W8: **The failure taxonomy (Table 8) has no formal inter-coder reliability.** Five categories were defined post-hoc from manual inspection. Without a codebook, independent coding by multiple annotators, and Cohen's kappa on the taxonomy assignment, the category boundaries and sample counts are subjective. The n=45 edit-revert and n=35 subtle-progress categories are small enough that a few reassignments would substantially change the per-category LF recall numbers.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: Can you provide bootstrap 95% CIs for all pairwise comparisons in Table 6? Is the GB vs. BGE gap (0.58 vs. 0.60, i.e., BGE actually beats GB by 2pp) significant?\n- Q2: What inter-annotator agreement was achieved on the 650-window gold set? Was annotation done by a single annotator or multiple? If multiple, how were disagreements resolved?\n- Q3: How were the 650 gold windows sampled from the full dataset? What is the overlap (if any) between gold windows and the training set used for structural models and probes?\n- Q4: For the retraining-on-gold experiment (GB: 0.63), what is the cross-validated estimate? Have you tried increasing gold training data or augmenting with high-confidence LF labels?\n- Q5: What performance does the LLM judge achieve with 5-shot prompting using gold-annotated examples? With chain-of-thought?\n- Q6: For the OpenHands cross-agent gold evaluation (Table 7, 0.52), are these true human annotations or LF-derived labels? If human, by whom and with what protocol?\n- Q7: What is the random-firing null baseline for temporal onset alignment? What does ±1 step alignment look like under a permutation test?\n- Q8: In Table 6, the convergence of all methods to 0.55–0.60 F1 is striking. Have you analyzed whether all methods make errors on the *same* windows (shared hard cases) or *different* windows (complementary errors)? An error correlation analysis or ensemble experiment would be informative.\n\n### Missing Experiments / Coverage Gaps\n\nAssessment against the 10 planned experiments:\n\n| # | Experiment | Status | Notes |\n|---|-----------|--------|-------|\n| 1 | Gold Set — LF-Gold Agreement | **Covered** (Table 1) | κ=0.38 overall, per-confidence and per-LF breakdowns present. Missing: IAA for the gold annotations themselves. |\n| 2 | Gold Set — Structural Features on Gold | **Covered** (Table 4) | LF-trained models evaluated on gold. Missing: gold-trained CV, learning curve. |\n| 3 | Gold Set — Probe on Gold | **Covered** (Table 5) | Three encoders evaluated. Missing: CIs. |\n| 4 | Gold Set — LLM Judge on Gold | **Covered** (Table 6) | Zero-shot only. Missing: few-shot, CoT variants. |\n| 5 | Gold Set — Temporal Onset Alignment | **Covered** (Table 10) | Missing: null baseline, stricter tolerance analysis. |\n| 6 | Gold Set — Failure Taxonomy | **Covered** (Table 8) | 5 categories. Missing: inter-coder reliability, formal codebook. |\n| 7 | Gold Set — Conf>=2 Label Reliability | **Covered** (Table 3) | 37% implied error rate is a strong finding. |\n| 8 | Gold Set — Cross-Agent Structural on Gold | **Partially covered** (Table 7) | OH gold provenance unclear; SWE gold validated. |\n| 9 | F1-Stag Per-Class Breakdown on Gold | **Covered** (Table 9) | Per-subtype model performance. Small n per category. |\n| 10 | Gold Set — TLM Reinterpretation | **Covered** (Table 11) | All three TLMs on gold. Clean negative result. |\n\nOverall coverage is ~85%. All 10 planned experiments are at least partially represented. The gaps are primarily in statistical rigor (CIs, significance tests), methodological documentation (IAA, sampling, codebook), and baseline fairness (LLM few-shot). No entire experiment is missing, but several are incomplete in execution.\n\n### Minor Issues\n- The paper uses \"F1\" ambiguously — sometimes macro-F1, sometimes stagnation-class F1. Table headers should be explicit.\n- Table 1 reports FNR=0.48 and FPR=0.22, indicating LFs miss nearly half of true stagnation. This deserves more discussion as it limits the ceiling for any LF-derived system.\n- The notation \"conf=1/2/3\" (number of agreeing LFs) could be confused with standard confidence scores. Consider \"agreement=1/2/3\" or \"LF consensus.\"\n- Table 3's \"implied error rate = 37%\" for conf>=2 is a critically important number that should appear in the abstract.\n- The phrase \"temporal alignment of noise is still noise\" is memorable but somewhat misleading — TWV and HMM do not merely align noise; they attempt to model temporal dependencies that the paper's own temporal dynamics analysis (Claim 2) argues are real. The tension between \"LFs have systematic temporal roles\" (Claim 2) and \"exploiting those temporal roles does not help\" (Claim 3) deserves more explicit discussion.\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 2/4\n- Contribution: 3/4\n- Presentation: 3/4\n- Overall: 3/6\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\nThe circular evaluation trap identification is a genuine contribution with transferable value to the weak supervision community. The failure taxonomy and TLM negative results add useful empirical findings. However, the absence of any statistical significance testing on a 650-sample gold set, the missing inter-annotator agreement, the unfairly weak LLM baseline, and the unclear OH gold provenance collectively prevent confidence that the claimed findings are robust. With CIs, IAA, a fair LLM baseline, and a null model for temporal alignment, this could become a solid Findings paper. In its current form, the evidence does not meet the rigor threshold for a top-venue long paper."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a042e721fa3f49567 (use SendMessage with to: 'a042e721fa3f49567' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 88657\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 140955</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b19c43c-1b27-4951-bda8-40543516a891","uuid":"eddf3ab0-d9ae-4167-bcb6-e2d15e262758","timestamp":"2026-05-14T19:28:31.328Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in a peer review for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%). Your focus areas are **methodology** and **experiments**. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"When Labeling Functions Lie: Circular Evaluation Traps in Weak Supervision for Agent Monitoring\"\n\n**Core claim**: Naive weak supervision for agent stagnation detection creates a circular evaluation trap. Structural features that encode LF input signals achieve near-perfect F1 (0.995) on LF-derived labels, but this measures fidelity to heuristic rules, not real stagnation detection. A 650-window human-annotated gold set reveals all models collapse to 0.55–0.60 F1 on gold labels.\n\n**Three contributions**:\n1. **Circular evaluation trap identification**: Structural models (GB/RF/LR) using features like command repetition ratio and file diff count achieve 0.995 F1 on LF labels but collapse to 0.55–0.61 on gold (−37 to −44 pp). These features are continuous versions of LF inputs, so the model reverse-engineers LF decision rules. Retraining on gold yields only 0.63 F1, showing structural features lack information capacity for semantic stagnation.\n\n2. **LF failure mode analysis**: A 5-category failure taxonomy from 650 gold windows:\n   - Semantic-diverse stagnation (n=82, LF recall 0.27): agent uses varied commands but repeats ineffective strategy\n   - Edit-revert loops (n=45, LF recall 0.38): file_diff fires on edits but misses reverts\n   - Diagnostic exploration / FP (n=58, LF precision 0.41): cmd_rep flags valid diagnostic repetition\n   - Subtle progress (n=35, LF recall 0.48): progress too incremental for threshold-based LFs\n   - Clear structural loops (n=55, LF recall 0.91): the only category LFs handle well (~20% of gold stagnation)\n\n3. **Temporal label model negative finding**: TWV (gold acc 0.60 vs MV 0.62), HMM (0.45), lag-aware HMM (0.58) all fail to beat MV on gold. \"Temporal alignment of noise is still noise.\"\n\n**Setup**: k=5 sliding windows over SWE-Agent and OpenHands trajectories on SWE-bench. Three LFs: cmd_rep, file_diff, outcome_bp. Majority vote with confidence 1–3. Prior work used conf≥2 subset (n=1,824) as test set.\n\n**Key tables**:\n- Table 1: LF-Gold agreement by confidence. Overall: N=650, acc=0.62, κ=0.38, FNR=0.48, FPR=0.22. conf=1: acc=0.41, κ=0.22. conf=2: acc=0.63, κ=0.42. conf=3: acc=0.72, κ=0.51.\n- Table 2: Per-LF gold agreement. cmd_rep κ=0.28/acc=0.55, file_diff κ=0.44/acc=0.68, outcome_bp κ=0.35/acc=0.60.\n- Table 3: Label reliability. conf≥2 implied error rate = 37%.\n- Table 4: Structural model collapse. GB: 0.9951→0.58 (−41.5pp), RF: 0.9951→0.55 (−44.0pp), LR: 0.9874→0.61 (−37.7pp), GB retrained on gold: 0.63.\n- Table 5: Probe collapse. Qwen3: 0.906→0.57 (−33.6pp), E5: 0.936→0.59 (−34.6pp), BGE: 0.941→0.60 (−34.1pp).\n- Table 6: All models on gold. Structural GB 0.58, RF 0.55, Probe Qwen3 0.57, BGE 0.60, LLM Judge 0.56. All converge to 0.55–0.60.\n- Table 7: Cross-agent. SWE in-domain 0.995→0.58, OH cross-agent 0.967→0.52 (−44.7pp).\n- Table 8: Failure taxonomy (5 categories as listed above).\n- Table 9: Stagnation F1 by subtype. Structural loops: struct 0.82, probe 0.78, LLM 0.75. Semantic-diverse: struct 0.25, probe 0.30, LLM 0.38. Edit-revert: struct 0.35, probe 0.33, LLM 0.30.\n- Table 10: Temporal onset alignment. 28% aligned (±1 step), LF leads 35%, lags 37%, mean offset 3.8 steps.\n- Table 11: TLM on gold. MV: acc 0.62/κ=0.38. TWV: 0.60/0.35. HMM: 0.45/0.15. Lag-aware HMM: 0.58/0.33.\n\n**Limitations section**: Gold set small (650), domain limited to SWE, only 2 agents, no inter-annotator agreement reported.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (for coverage assessment)\n\nThe full experiment plan includes 10 imagined experiments:\n1. Gold Set — LF-Gold Agreement (κ analysis, per-confidence, per-LF, failure taxonomy)\n2. Gold Set — Structural Features on Gold (LF-trained models evaluated on gold)\n3. Gold Set — Probe on Gold (GRU probes on gold)\n4. Gold Set — LLM Judge on Gold (Claude Sonnet on gold, convergence table)\n5. Gold Set — Temporal Onset Alignment (onset offset analysis)\n6. Gold Set — Failure Taxonomy (5-category qualitative analysis)\n7. Gold Set — Conf≥2 Label Reliability (test set reliability analysis)\n8. Gold Set — Cross-Agent Structural on Gold\n9. F1-Stag Per-Class Breakdown on Gold (per-subtype model performance)\n10. Gold Set — TLM Reinterpretation (temporal label models on gold)\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the experimental design rigorous? Are there flaws in the gold set construction, evaluation protocol, or statistical analysis?\n2. **Experimental completeness**: How well does the paper cover the 10 planned experiments? Are key results missing or underspecified?\n3. **Statistical rigor**: Are confidence intervals, significance tests, or bootstrap analyses reported? Is the gold set size sufficient for the claims made?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is the methodology described in sufficient detail?\n5. **Strengths and weaknesses**: Specific, actionable points.\n\nOutput your review in this exact format:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methodology and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Missing Experiments / Coverage Gaps\n[Assessment of which of the 10 planned experiments are covered vs missing]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Any minor points]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/4\n- Contribution: X/4\n- Presentation: X/4\n- Overall: X/6\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n","agentId":"a042e721fa3f49567","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nBased on my thorough analysis of the project materials — the paper summary, experimental results, decision log, prior dream reviews, writing plan, and paper framing — here is my review.\n\n## R1 Review: Methodology and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper identifies a \"circular evaluation trap\" in weak supervision for agent stagnation detection, demonstrating that structural features achieve near-perfect F1 (0.995) on LF-derived labels but collapse to 0.55–0.60 on a 650-window human-annotated gold set. A 5-category failure taxonomy and negative results on three temporal label model implementations (TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM) are presented. The core message — that high scores on LF-derived labels measure fidelity to heuristic rules rather than real stagnation detection — is a genuine methodological insight, but the paper suffers from serious statistical gaps, an insufficiently validated gold set, and incomplete experimental analysis.\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: **The circular evaluation trap is the paper's strongest and most transferable contribution.** The mechanistic explanation is clear: structural features are continuous relaxations of LF inputs, so training on LF-derived labels and evaluating on LF-derived labels produces a tautological feedback loop. The 37–44pp collapse from LF-eval to gold-eval (Table 4) is a striking demonstration. This finding generalizes to any weak supervision pipeline where features correlate with the label-generating heuristics.\n- S2: **Comprehensive multi-method comparison on a shared gold set.** Evaluating structural classifiers (GB/RF/LR), three neural probe encoders (Qwen3/E5/BGE), and an LLM judge all on the same 650-window gold set with consistent metrics (Table 6) is methodologically clean and provides an apples-to-apples comparison that the field needs.\n- S3: **The failure taxonomy (Table 8) is descriptively valuable.** Decomposing LF failures into 5 categories with per-category LF recall (ranging from 0.27 for semantic-diverse stagnation to 0.91 for clear structural loops) reveals where the weak supervision pipeline breaks down. The finding that LFs only handle ~20% of gold stagnation well (the \"clear structural loops\" category) is a compelling indictment of naive LF-based approaches.\n- S4: **Honest and well-motivated negative results on TLMs.** Three distinct implementations (TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM) all fail to beat majority vote on gold labels (Table 11). The observation \"temporal alignment of noise is still noise\" is pithy and well-supported by the data. The failure analysis pointing to the low-conflict 3-LF regime is a useful constraint for future research.\n- S5: **The convergence finding (Table 6) is noteworthy.** All methods — structural, neural, LLM judge — converge to 0.55–0.60 F1 on gold, suggesting a shared ceiling imposed by the difficulty of the task and/or limitations in all current feature representations. This is a genuinely surprising finding that deserves more prominence.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: **No confidence intervals, significance tests, or bootstrap analyses anywhere in the paper.** This is the most critical gap. The core claim rests on differences of 1–5pp across methods on a 650-sample gold set (e.g., GB 0.58 vs. BGE 0.60 vs. LLM judge 0.56 in Table 6). Without McNemar's test or bootstrap 95% CIs, it is impossible to determine whether these differences are statistically meaningful or noise. For an empirical analysis paper at a top venue, this is a disqualifying omission. Furthermore, the gold set contains unbalanced subgroup sizes in the failure taxonomy (n=35 to n=82 per category), and the per-subtype F1 numbers in Table 9 (e.g., structural 0.25 vs. LLM 0.38 on semantic-diverse stagnation) are reported without any uncertainty quantification on samples this small.\n- W2: **No inter-annotator agreement (IAA) is reported for the gold set.** The paper acknowledges this in the Limitations section but does not report Cohen's kappa, percent agreement, or any IAA metric. For a 650-window gold set that serves as the foundation for every claim in the paper, this is a critical methodological hole. Without IAA, we cannot distinguish annotation noise from genuine model failures. If IAA kappa is, say, 0.65, then the gold F1 ceiling may itself be an artifact of annotation disagreement rather than a property of the methods under evaluation. The decision log reveals that earlier phases of the project struggled with label quality (D004: raw agreement only 53%, D006: 81.5% after excluding uncertain cases), raising legitimate concerns about whether the final gold annotations are reliable.\n- W3: **Gold set construction and sampling strategy are underspecified.** How were the 650 windows sampled? Were they stratified by confidence level, trajectory length, agent type, or LF agreement pattern? The distribution across the 5 failure categories (Table 8) suggests some stratification occurred (otherwise semantic-diverse stagnation at n=82 seems overrepresented relative to the base rate), but this is not documented. Selection bias in the gold set directly affects all downstream conclusions. Were any of these windows used during LF development or model training? The paper does not address potential contamination.\n- W4: **The retraining-on-gold experiment (GB retrained: 0.63 F1) is insufficiently analyzed.** This is presented as evidence that \"structural features lack information capacity for semantic stagnation,\" but with only 650 training samples, the poor performance could simply reflect insufficient data rather than inherent feature limitations. A learning curve (training on 100/200/400/650 gold samples) or cross-validated estimate would be needed to disentangle the two explanations. The project's own data-scaling experiments on LF labels showed monotonic improvement from 10% to 100% data (data-scaling-v2), so the 650-sample regime is far below where structural models saturate.\n- W5: **LLM judge baseline is unfairly weak.** Only zero-shot classification is tested (Table 6, F1=0.56). No few-shot prompting, no chain-of-thought, no instruction-tuned variants, no other LLMs. The claim that structural features \"decisively surpass\" LLM judges rests on a single zero-shot prompt. Given that few-shot prompting routinely improves LLM classification by 10–20pp, the 2pp gap between LLM judge (0.56) and structural GB (0.58) on gold could easily reverse with a fairer LLM baseline. This undermines one of the paper's headline claims.\n- W6: **Cross-agent evaluation (Table 7) lacks gold validation.** The OpenHands cross-agent results (SWE in-domain 0.995→0.58 on gold, OH cross-agent 0.967→0.52 on gold) appear to use gold labels for the SWE side but LF-derived labels for the OH side (the OH number 0.52 is described as gold, but the project's own records show no OH gold annotation was completed). If the OH \"gold\" evaluation actually uses LF-derived labels corrected by the same circularity the paper critiques, this is a serious methodological inconsistency. If genuine OH gold labels exist, the annotation protocol should be documented with the same rigor as the SWE gold set.\n- W7: **Temporal onset alignment (Table 10) lacks a null baseline.** 28% of onsets align within ±1 step — but what is the expected alignment under random firing? With k=5 windows and systematic class imbalance, a random null could produce non-trivial alignment by chance. Without this calibration, the 28% figure is uninterpretable. Additionally, the mean offset of 3.8 steps on k=5 windows represents a 76% window-width misalignment, which seems to undermine the claim that LF temporal dynamics are \"systematic.\"\n- W8: **The failure taxonomy (Table 8) has no formal inter-coder reliability.** Five categories were defined post-hoc from manual inspection. Without a codebook, independent coding by multiple annotators, and Cohen's kappa on the taxonomy assignment, the category boundaries and sample counts are subjective. The n=45 edit-revert and n=35 subtle-progress categories are small enough that a few reassignments would substantially change the per-category LF recall numbers.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: Can you provide bootstrap 95% CIs for all pairwise comparisons in Table 6? Is the GB vs. BGE gap (0.58 vs. 0.60, i.e., BGE actually beats GB by 2pp) significant?\n- Q2: What inter-annotator agreement was achieved on the 650-window gold set? Was annotation done by a single annotator or multiple? If multiple, how were disagreements resolved?\n- Q3: How were the 650 gold windows sampled from the full dataset? What is the overlap (if any) between gold windows and the training set used for structural models and probes?\n- Q4: For the retraining-on-gold experiment (GB: 0.63), what is the cross-validated estimate? Have you tried increasing gold training data or augmenting with high-confidence LF labels?\n- Q5: What performance does the LLM judge achieve with 5-shot prompting using gold-annotated examples? With chain-of-thought?\n- Q6: For the OpenHands cross-agent gold evaluation (Table 7, 0.52), are these true human annotations or LF-derived labels? If human, by whom and with what protocol?\n- Q7: What is the random-firing null baseline for temporal onset alignment? What does ±1 step alignment look like under a permutation test?\n- Q8: In Table 6, the convergence of all methods to 0.55–0.60 F1 is striking. Have you analyzed whether all methods make errors on the *same* windows (shared hard cases) or *different* windows (complementary errors)? An error correlation analysis or ensemble experiment would be informative.\n\n### Missing Experiments / Coverage Gaps\n\nAssessment against the 10 planned experiments:\n\n| # | Experiment | Status | Notes |\n|---|-----------|--------|-------|\n| 1 | Gold Set — LF-Gold Agreement | **Covered** (Table 1) | κ=0.38 overall, per-confidence and per-LF breakdowns present. Missing: IAA for the gold annotations themselves. |\n| 2 | Gold Set — Structural Features on Gold | **Covered** (Table 4) | LF-trained models evaluated on gold. Missing: gold-trained CV, learning curve. |\n| 3 | Gold Set — Probe on Gold | **Covered** (Table 5) | Three encoders evaluated. Missing: CIs. |\n| 4 | Gold Set — LLM Judge on Gold | **Covered** (Table 6) | Zero-shot only. Missing: few-shot, CoT variants. |\n| 5 | Gold Set — Temporal Onset Alignment | **Covered** (Table 10) | Missing: null baseline, stricter tolerance analysis. |\n| 6 | Gold Set — Failure Taxonomy | **Covered** (Table 8) | 5 categories. Missing: inter-coder reliability, formal codebook. |\n| 7 | Gold Set — Conf>=2 Label Reliability | **Covered** (Table 3) | 37% implied error rate is a strong finding. |\n| 8 | Gold Set — Cross-Agent Structural on Gold | **Partially covered** (Table 7) | OH gold provenance unclear; SWE gold validated. |\n| 9 | F1-Stag Per-Class Breakdown on Gold | **Covered** (Table 9) | Per-subtype model performance. Small n per category. |\n| 10 | Gold Set — TLM Reinterpretation | **Covered** (Table 11) | All three TLMs on gold. Clean negative result. |\n\nOverall coverage is ~85%. All 10 planned experiments are at least partially represented. The gaps are primarily in statistical rigor (CIs, significance tests), methodological documentation (IAA, sampling, codebook), and baseline fairness (LLM few-shot). No entire experiment is missing, but several are incomplete in execution.\n\n### Minor Issues\n- The paper uses \"F1\" ambiguously — sometimes macro-F1, sometimes stagnation-class F1. Table headers should be explicit.\n- Table 1 reports FNR=0.48 and FPR=0.22, indicating LFs miss nearly half of true stagnation. This deserves more discussion as it limits the ceiling for any LF-derived system.\n- The notation \"conf=1/2/3\" (number of agreeing LFs) could be confused with standard confidence scores. Consider \"agreement=1/2/3\" or \"LF consensus.\"\n- Table 3's \"implied error rate = 37%\" for conf>=2 is a critically important number that should appear in the abstract.\n- The phrase \"temporal alignment of noise is still noise\" is memorable but somewhat misleading — TWV and HMM do not merely align noise; they attempt to model temporal dependencies that the paper's own temporal dynamics analysis (Claim 2) argues are real. The tension between \"LFs have systematic temporal roles\" (Claim 2) and \"exploiting those temporal roles does not help\" (Claim 3) deserves more explicit discussion.\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 2/4\n- Contribution: 3/4\n- Presentation: 3/4\n- Overall: 3/6\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\nThe circular evaluation trap identification is a genuine contribution with transferable value to the weak supervision community. The failure taxonomy and TLM negative results add useful empirical findings. However, the absence of any statistical significance testing on a 650-sample gold set, the missing inter-annotator agreement, the unfairly weak LLM baseline, and the unclear OH gold provenance collectively prevent confidence that the claimed findings are robust. With CIs, IAA, a fair LLM baseline, and a null model for temporal alignment, this could become a solid Findings paper. In its current form, the evidence does not meet the rigor threshold for a top-venue long paper."}],"totalDurationMs":140955,"totalTokens":88657,"totalToolUseCount":20,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":698,"cache_read_input_tokens":84574,"output_tokens":3382,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":698},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3382,"cache_read_input_tokens":84574,"cache_creation_input_tokens":698,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":698,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":12}}}
