{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PuqCM1RDwcW4k2cPdFot2U","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have comprehensive understanding of the paper and its full experimental history. Let me write the R2 review.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper identifies a \"circular evaluation trap\" in weak supervision for agent stagnation detection: structural features that encode LF input signals achieve near-perfect F1 (0.995) on LF-derived labels but collapse to 0.55--0.60 on a 650-window human-annotated gold set. It provides a 5-category LF failure taxonomy and shows that three temporal label model implementations (TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM) all fail to beat majority vote. The paper is reframed around the negative/diagnostic finding rather than proposing a new method.\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: **The circular evaluation quantification is a genuine, transferable contribution.** The demonstration that structural models drop 37--44pp from LF-derived to gold evaluation, while the mechanistic explanation (features approximate continuous versions of LF inputs, so models reverse-engineer heuristic rules) is clean and generalizable to any WS pipeline where features and LFs share input signals. The recommendation for feature-LF independence audits when LF-label F1 > 0.95 is actionable. This is the kind of methodological finding that changes practitioner behavior.\n\n- S2: **The 5-category failure taxonomy is concrete and useful.** Identifying that only ~20% of gold stagnation falls into \"clear structural loops\" (where LFs work well, recall 0.91), while 30% is \"semantic-diverse stagnation\" (LF recall 0.27), provides a quantified decomposition of where weak supervision breaks. The per-subtype F1 analysis (Table 9) showing all methods collapse on semantic-diverse stagnation is informative. The categories are specific enough to guide future LF design.\n\n- S3: **Honest and well-structured negative result on TLMs.** Three distinct implementations failing, combined with the mechanistic explanation (low-conflict 3-LF regime, 95.1% agreement at conf>=2), provides useful constraints for future temporal label model research. The \"temporal alignment of noise is still noise\" framing is memorable and accurate. The finding that TWV degrades downstream classifiers by up to 21.7pp is particularly striking.\n\n- S4: **The gold set validation is the right experimental design** for addressing circularity concerns. The paper practices what it preaches: it identifies circular evaluation as a methodological problem and then constructs an independent evaluation to break the circle. The LF-Gold kappa=0.38 overall is a sobering number that validates the paper's central thesis.\n\n- S5: **Cross-agent analysis reveals non-obvious LF-architecture interactions.** The cmd_rep role reversal between SWE-Agent (leading) and OpenHands (lagging, 92.3% firing rate, 71% FP), with removal improving OH F1 by +21.6pp, is a concrete empirical finding with practical implications for WS pipeline deployment across agent architectures.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: **The core finding -- that features correlated with label-generating heuristics predict those labels -- is not surprising to the WS community.** The data programming literature (Ratner et al. 2016, 2017) explicitly models the relationship between LF noise and downstream model behavior. The circular evaluation trap is a well-understood concern: the Snorkel papers discuss using held-out gold sets precisely for this reason. The paper's contribution is a concrete, quantified case study, but it reads more as a cautionary tale than a novel finding. The \"all models collapse to 0.55--0.60 on gold\" result is the genuine surprise, but the magnitude of collapse depends heavily on the quality of the 3 LFs and the domain -- it is unclear how much generalizes beyond this specific setup.\n\n- W2: **Gold set limitations undermine the paper's strongest claims.** 650 windows from a single domain (SWE), 2 agents, and no inter-annotator kappa reported. The paper's entire thesis rests on gold evaluation, yet the gold set itself is not rigorously characterized. What is the inter-annotator agreement? How were annotators trained? What guidelines were used for ambiguous cases (e.g., \"diagnostic exploration\" vs. \"stagnation\")? Without inter-annotator kappa >= 0.75 and explicit annotation protocols, the gold-derived numbers carry unknown measurement error. The failure taxonomy subtypes have small N (e.g., n=35 for \"subtle progress,\" n=45 for \"edit-revert loops\"), limiting statistical power for per-subtype claims.\n\n- W3: **No statistical significance tests on any comparison.** The paper reports model performance on gold as 0.55--0.60 F1 for all methods, suggesting they are statistically indistinguishable. But no confidence intervals, no McNemar's test, no bootstrap analysis. If all models genuinely converge to the same gold F1 range, the paper's claim that \"structural features are best on LF-derived labels but equal on gold\" needs formal statistical support. Conversely, if any method is significantly better on gold, the paper's \"all methods collapse\" narrative may be oversimplified.\n\n- W4: **The negative TLM finding is expected given the experimental regime.** With only 3 LFs and 95.1% agreement at conf>=2, there is almost nothing for a temporal model to correct. Standard WS benchmarks (WRENCH, Zhang et al. 2021) use 10+ LFs. The paper acknowledges this but does not attempt a more informative regime (6-8 LFs) that could distinguish \"TLMs cannot help on sequential agent data\" from \"TLMs cannot help with 3 low-conflict LFs.\" This weakens the generalizability of the negative finding considerably.\n\n- W5: **The paper's scope is narrow: 2 SE agents on SWE-bench.** The title and framing (\"Weak Supervision for Agent Monitoring\") imply broad applicability, but all experimental evidence is from coding agents. Structural features like \"file diff frequency\" and \"unique command ratio\" are SE-specific. Whether the circular evaluation trap and the failure taxonomy transfer to web navigation, scientific discovery, or conversational agents is entirely unknown. The paper should be more explicit about this scope limitation in the title or abstract.\n\n- W6: **The temporal onset alignment claim (28% in Table 10) is weak and lacks a null baseline.** Only 28% of stagnation windows show temporal onset alignment between LF-derived and gold boundaries. No random-firing null distribution is provided to calibrate whether this exceeds chance. On k=5 windows, even random alignment could produce non-trivial overlap.\n\n- W7: **The paper does not attempt to train on gold labels.** A natural experiment -- training structural/probe models on gold labels via cross-validation -- would separate the effect of training signal quality from model capacity. If models trained on gold labels achieve substantially higher gold F1, the paper's narrative strengthens (\"LF labels are the bottleneck\"). If not, the ceiling is model capacity, not label quality. This omission leaves a gap in the analysis.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: What is the inter-annotator kappa on the gold set? How were disagreements resolved? This is critical for interpreting all gold-derived results.\n- Q2: Are the 0.55--0.60 gold F1 values for different models statistically distinguishable? Please provide bootstrap CIs or McNemar's test.\n- Q3: Have you considered expanding to 6-8 LFs to provide a more informative test of temporal label models? The 3-LF low-conflict regime makes the negative finding somewhat trivial.\n- Q4: What happens if you train models directly on gold labels (5-fold CV on 650 samples)? Does gold-trained structural GB exceed LF-trained GB on gold evaluation?\n- Q5: How do you distinguish your circular evaluation finding from the standard recommendation in the Snorkel literature to always validate on a gold dev set? What is genuinely new here beyond the specific quantification?\n- Q6: For the failure taxonomy, was the categorization performed by the same annotators as the gold labels? Was inter-rater reliability measured for the taxonomy categories?\n\n### Related Work Gaps\nThe related work section has significant omissions that weaken the paper's positioning:\n\n1. **Safranchik et al. (AAAI 2020), \"Weakly Supervised Sequence Tagging from Noisy Rules\"**: This is the most direct methodological predecessor for the TLM attempt -- a linked HMM for sequential weak supervision. Its omission prevents the paper from contextualizing why HMM-based approaches fail here vs. succeed in NER.\n\n2. **Sala et al. (NeurIPS 2019), \"Multi-Resolution Weak Supervision for Sequential Data\"**: Direct precedent for weak supervision on sequential data with temporal structure. Essential for the temporal dynamics claims.\n\n3. **Fu et al. (ICML 2020), FlyingSquid**: Core WS label model paper. The paper compares against MV but not FlyingSquid or other label models (Dawid-Skene, MeTaL).\n\n4. **Zhang et al. (NeurIPS 2021), WRENCH benchmark**: Standard WS evaluation benchmark. Contextualizes the 3-LF regime as unusually small.\n\n5. **George et al. (2025), \"Unsupervised Cycle Detection in Agentic Applications\" (arXiv 2511.10650)**: Most directly comparable prior work on cycle/stagnation detection combining structural and embedding approaches. Significant omission given the structural-vs-semantic theme.\n\n6. **Nguyen et al. (ACL 2017), HMM-Crowd**: HMM for label aggregation in sequential data -- directly relevant to the HMM-TLM approach.\n\n7. **Lightman et al. (2023), \"Let's Verify Step by Step\"**: Foundational PRM work predating AgentPRM. The PRM discussion should reference the origin of process-level verification.\n\n8. The paper frames majority vote as the only i.i.d. label model alternative without discussing more sophisticated aggregation (Dawid-Skene EM, Snorkel's generative model, FlyingSquid's triplet method). This makes the TLM negative finding less informative -- did more sophisticated i.i.d. models also fail?\n\n### Minor Issues\n- Table 1 (LF-Gold kappa=0.38 overall) and the abstract should explicitly state whether this is Cohen's kappa, Fleiss' kappa, or another variant.\n- The paper should clarify whether the 650 gold windows are a subset of the training data, the test data, or independently sampled. If they overlap with training data, there is a train-test contamination concern.\n- The \"edit-revert loops\" category (n=45, LF recall 0.38) is interesting but under-analyzed. Are these detectable by a specialized LF? This seems like low-hanging fruit for improving the WS pipeline.\n- Section ordering: the failure taxonomy (contribution 2) should come before the TLM results (contribution 3), as the taxonomy motivates why TLMs might help.\n- The paper would benefit from a figure showing the circular evaluation trap visually -- e.g., a Venn diagram or flow diagram showing the shared information between features and LFs.\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 3/4\n- Contribution: 2/4\n- Presentation: 3/4\n- Overall: 3/6\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\nThe paper is methodologically sound and honestly reported, but the novelty is below the bar for a top ML venue. The circular evaluation finding, while well-quantified, is not surprising to the WS community. The failure taxonomy is useful but small-N. The TLM negative result is weakened by the low-conflict 3-LF regime. The narrow scope (2 SE agents) limits significance claims. With stronger statistical analysis, expanded LF coverage, a fairer LLM baseline, and broader domain evaluation, this could become a solid Findings-track or workshop contribution. In its current form, it reads as a careful but incremental empirical study in a niche application domain."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: afd49a7379d279938 (use SendMessage with to: 'afd49a7379d279938' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 87384\ntool_uses: 17\nduration_ms: 124778</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b19c43c-1b27-4951-bda8-40543516a891","uuid":"62186a8a-ff5d-4bc8-a88b-be679f79073b","timestamp":"2026-05-14T19:28:35.105Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in a peer review for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%). Your focus areas are **novelty/significance** and **related work positioning**. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"When Labeling Functions Lie: Circular Evaluation Traps in Weak Supervision for Agent Monitoring\"\n\n**Core claim**: Naive weak supervision for agent stagnation detection creates a circular evaluation trap. Structural features that encode LF input signals achieve near-perfect F1 (0.995) on LF-derived labels, but this measures fidelity to heuristic rules, not real stagnation detection. A 650-window human-annotated gold set reveals all models collapse to 0.55–0.60 F1 on gold labels.\n\n**Three contributions**:\n1. **Circular evaluation trap identification**: Structural models (GB/RF/LR) collapse from 0.995 to 0.55–0.61 F1 on gold (−37 to −44 pp). Features are continuous versions of LF inputs, so models reverse-engineer LF rules.\n\n2. **LF failure mode analysis**: 5-category failure taxonomy from gold set:\n   - Semantic-diverse stagnation (n=82, LF recall 0.27)\n   - Edit-revert loops (n=45, LF recall 0.38)\n   - Diagnostic exploration / FP (n=58, LF precision 0.41)\n   - Subtle progress (n=35, LF recall 0.48)\n   - Clear structural loops (n=55, LF recall 0.91) — only ~20% of gold stagnation\n\n3. **Temporal label model negative finding**: TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM all fail to beat MV on gold. \"Temporal alignment of noise is still noise.\"\n\n**Related Work section** covers three areas:\n- **Agent Monitoring and Stagnation Detection**: References SlopCodeBench, cycle detectors (CDCS, CDSA, SpecRA, TrajAD), PRMs (AgentPRM), self-reflection (Reflexion, LATS, Tree of Thoughts). Differentiates by using window-level weak supervision without outcome labels.\n- **Weak Supervision and Data Programming**: References Snorkel framework, label models (MV, data-aware). Notes that sequential/temporal data and LF-agent architecture interactions are unexplored. Claims \"first empirical study of weak supervision failure modes specific to sequential agent data.\"\n- **Evaluation Methodology**: Notes circular evaluation is a known NLP concern. Claims to provide \"a concrete, quantified case study\" in agent monitoring.\n\n**Key results**:\n- Table 1: LF-Gold κ=0.38 overall\n- Table 6: All models converge to 0.55–0.60 on gold (from 0.663–0.995 on LF)\n- Table 8: Failure taxonomy with 5 categories\n- Table 9: Per-subtype F1 showing all methods fail on semantic-diverse stagnation\n- Table 10: Only 28% temporal onset alignment\n- Table 11: TLM all fail on gold\n\n**Discussion recommends**: (1) feature-LF independence audits, (2) mandatory gold-set validation when LF-label F1 > 0.95, (3) reporting LF-gold κ. Future LFs should operate at strategy level, not command level.\n\n**Limitations**: Gold set 650 windows, SWE domain only, 2 agents, no inter-annotator κ.\n\n**Paper is 10 pages, anonymous.**\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is the circular evaluation trap finding? Is it surprising to the WS/ML community? Is the failure taxonomy a genuine contribution?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important baselines or related efforts missing? Is the positioning accurate?\n3. **Significance and impact**: Would this paper change how people build WS pipelines for agent monitoring? Is the audience large enough?\n4. **Narrative and framing**: Is the paper well-structured? Does the \"negative result\" framing work? Is it a methodology/analysis paper or an empirical contribution?\n5. **Strengths and weaknesses**: Specific, actionable points.\n\nOutput your review in this exact format:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n[Missing references or positioning issues]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Any minor points]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/4\n- Contribution: X/4\n- Presentation: X/4\n- Overall: X/6\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n","agentId":"afd49a7379d279938","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have comprehensive understanding of the paper and its full experimental history. Let me write the R2 review.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper identifies a \"circular evaluation trap\" in weak supervision for agent stagnation detection: structural features that encode LF input signals achieve near-perfect F1 (0.995) on LF-derived labels but collapse to 0.55--0.60 on a 650-window human-annotated gold set. It provides a 5-category LF failure taxonomy and shows that three temporal label model implementations (TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM) all fail to beat majority vote. The paper is reframed around the negative/diagnostic finding rather than proposing a new method.\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: **The circular evaluation quantification is a genuine, transferable contribution.** The demonstration that structural models drop 37--44pp from LF-derived to gold evaluation, while the mechanistic explanation (features approximate continuous versions of LF inputs, so models reverse-engineer heuristic rules) is clean and generalizable to any WS pipeline where features and LFs share input signals. The recommendation for feature-LF independence audits when LF-label F1 > 0.95 is actionable. This is the kind of methodological finding that changes practitioner behavior.\n\n- S2: **The 5-category failure taxonomy is concrete and useful.** Identifying that only ~20% of gold stagnation falls into \"clear structural loops\" (where LFs work well, recall 0.91), while 30% is \"semantic-diverse stagnation\" (LF recall 0.27), provides a quantified decomposition of where weak supervision breaks. The per-subtype F1 analysis (Table 9) showing all methods collapse on semantic-diverse stagnation is informative. The categories are specific enough to guide future LF design.\n\n- S3: **Honest and well-structured negative result on TLMs.** Three distinct implementations failing, combined with the mechanistic explanation (low-conflict 3-LF regime, 95.1% agreement at conf>=2), provides useful constraints for future temporal label model research. The \"temporal alignment of noise is still noise\" framing is memorable and accurate. The finding that TWV degrades downstream classifiers by up to 21.7pp is particularly striking.\n\n- S4: **The gold set validation is the right experimental design** for addressing circularity concerns. The paper practices what it preaches: it identifies circular evaluation as a methodological problem and then constructs an independent evaluation to break the circle. The LF-Gold kappa=0.38 overall is a sobering number that validates the paper's central thesis.\n\n- S5: **Cross-agent analysis reveals non-obvious LF-architecture interactions.** The cmd_rep role reversal between SWE-Agent (leading) and OpenHands (lagging, 92.3% firing rate, 71% FP), with removal improving OH F1 by +21.6pp, is a concrete empirical finding with practical implications for WS pipeline deployment across agent architectures.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: **The core finding -- that features correlated with label-generating heuristics predict those labels -- is not surprising to the WS community.** The data programming literature (Ratner et al. 2016, 2017) explicitly models the relationship between LF noise and downstream model behavior. The circular evaluation trap is a well-understood concern: the Snorkel papers discuss using held-out gold sets precisely for this reason. The paper's contribution is a concrete, quantified case study, but it reads more as a cautionary tale than a novel finding. The \"all models collapse to 0.55--0.60 on gold\" result is the genuine surprise, but the magnitude of collapse depends heavily on the quality of the 3 LFs and the domain -- it is unclear how much generalizes beyond this specific setup.\n\n- W2: **Gold set limitations undermine the paper's strongest claims.** 650 windows from a single domain (SWE), 2 agents, and no inter-annotator kappa reported. The paper's entire thesis rests on gold evaluation, yet the gold set itself is not rigorously characterized. What is the inter-annotator agreement? How were annotators trained? What guidelines were used for ambiguous cases (e.g., \"diagnostic exploration\" vs. \"stagnation\")? Without inter-annotator kappa >= 0.75 and explicit annotation protocols, the gold-derived numbers carry unknown measurement error. The failure taxonomy subtypes have small N (e.g., n=35 for \"subtle progress,\" n=45 for \"edit-revert loops\"), limiting statistical power for per-subtype claims.\n\n- W3: **No statistical significance tests on any comparison.** The paper reports model performance on gold as 0.55--0.60 F1 for all methods, suggesting they are statistically indistinguishable. But no confidence intervals, no McNemar's test, no bootstrap analysis. If all models genuinely converge to the same gold F1 range, the paper's claim that \"structural features are best on LF-derived labels but equal on gold\" needs formal statistical support. Conversely, if any method is significantly better on gold, the paper's \"all methods collapse\" narrative may be oversimplified.\n\n- W4: **The negative TLM finding is expected given the experimental regime.** With only 3 LFs and 95.1% agreement at conf>=2, there is almost nothing for a temporal model to correct. Standard WS benchmarks (WRENCH, Zhang et al. 2021) use 10+ LFs. The paper acknowledges this but does not attempt a more informative regime (6-8 LFs) that could distinguish \"TLMs cannot help on sequential agent data\" from \"TLMs cannot help with 3 low-conflict LFs.\" This weakens the generalizability of the negative finding considerably.\n\n- W5: **The paper's scope is narrow: 2 SE agents on SWE-bench.** The title and framing (\"Weak Supervision for Agent Monitoring\") imply broad applicability, but all experimental evidence is from coding agents. Structural features like \"file diff frequency\" and \"unique command ratio\" are SE-specific. Whether the circular evaluation trap and the failure taxonomy transfer to web navigation, scientific discovery, or conversational agents is entirely unknown. The paper should be more explicit about this scope limitation in the title or abstract.\n\n- W6: **The temporal onset alignment claim (28% in Table 10) is weak and lacks a null baseline.** Only 28% of stagnation windows show temporal onset alignment between LF-derived and gold boundaries. No random-firing null distribution is provided to calibrate whether this exceeds chance. On k=5 windows, even random alignment could produce non-trivial overlap.\n\n- W7: **The paper does not attempt to train on gold labels.** A natural experiment -- training structural/probe models on gold labels via cross-validation -- would separate the effect of training signal quality from model capacity. If models trained on gold labels achieve substantially higher gold F1, the paper's narrative strengthens (\"LF labels are the bottleneck\"). If not, the ceiling is model capacity, not label quality. This omission leaves a gap in the analysis.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: What is the inter-annotator kappa on the gold set? How were disagreements resolved? This is critical for interpreting all gold-derived results.\n- Q2: Are the 0.55--0.60 gold F1 values for different models statistically distinguishable? Please provide bootstrap CIs or McNemar's test.\n- Q3: Have you considered expanding to 6-8 LFs to provide a more informative test of temporal label models? The 3-LF low-conflict regime makes the negative finding somewhat trivial.\n- Q4: What happens if you train models directly on gold labels (5-fold CV on 650 samples)? Does gold-trained structural GB exceed LF-trained GB on gold evaluation?\n- Q5: How do you distinguish your circular evaluation finding from the standard recommendation in the Snorkel literature to always validate on a gold dev set? What is genuinely new here beyond the specific quantification?\n- Q6: For the failure taxonomy, was the categorization performed by the same annotators as the gold labels? Was inter-rater reliability measured for the taxonomy categories?\n\n### Related Work Gaps\nThe related work section has significant omissions that weaken the paper's positioning:\n\n1. **Safranchik et al. (AAAI 2020), \"Weakly Supervised Sequence Tagging from Noisy Rules\"**: This is the most direct methodological predecessor for the TLM attempt -- a linked HMM for sequential weak supervision. Its omission prevents the paper from contextualizing why HMM-based approaches fail here vs. succeed in NER.\n\n2. **Sala et al. (NeurIPS 2019), \"Multi-Resolution Weak Supervision for Sequential Data\"**: Direct precedent for weak supervision on sequential data with temporal structure. Essential for the temporal dynamics claims.\n\n3. **Fu et al. (ICML 2020), FlyingSquid**: Core WS label model paper. The paper compares against MV but not FlyingSquid or other label models (Dawid-Skene, MeTaL).\n\n4. **Zhang et al. (NeurIPS 2021), WRENCH benchmark**: Standard WS evaluation benchmark. Contextualizes the 3-LF regime as unusually small.\n\n5. **George et al. (2025), \"Unsupervised Cycle Detection in Agentic Applications\" (arXiv 2511.10650)**: Most directly comparable prior work on cycle/stagnation detection combining structural and embedding approaches. Significant omission given the structural-vs-semantic theme.\n\n6. **Nguyen et al. (ACL 2017), HMM-Crowd**: HMM for label aggregation in sequential data -- directly relevant to the HMM-TLM approach.\n\n7. **Lightman et al. (2023), \"Let's Verify Step by Step\"**: Foundational PRM work predating AgentPRM. The PRM discussion should reference the origin of process-level verification.\n\n8. The paper frames majority vote as the only i.i.d. label model alternative without discussing more sophisticated aggregation (Dawid-Skene EM, Snorkel's generative model, FlyingSquid's triplet method). This makes the TLM negative finding less informative -- did more sophisticated i.i.d. models also fail?\n\n### Minor Issues\n- Table 1 (LF-Gold kappa=0.38 overall) and the abstract should explicitly state whether this is Cohen's kappa, Fleiss' kappa, or another variant.\n- The paper should clarify whether the 650 gold windows are a subset of the training data, the test data, or independently sampled. If they overlap with training data, there is a train-test contamination concern.\n- The \"edit-revert loops\" category (n=45, LF recall 0.38) is interesting but under-analyzed. Are these detectable by a specialized LF? This seems like low-hanging fruit for improving the WS pipeline.\n- Section ordering: the failure taxonomy (contribution 2) should come before the TLM results (contribution 3), as the taxonomy motivates why TLMs might help.\n- The paper would benefit from a figure showing the circular evaluation trap visually -- e.g., a Venn diagram or flow diagram showing the shared information between features and LFs.\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 3/4\n- Contribution: 2/4\n- Presentation: 3/4\n- Overall: 3/6\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\nThe paper is methodologically sound and honestly reported, but the novelty is below the bar for a top ML venue. The circular evaluation finding, while well-quantified, is not surprising to the WS community. The failure taxonomy is useful but small-N. The TLM negative result is weakened by the low-conflict 3-LF regime. The narrow scope (2 SE agents) limits significance claims. With stronger statistical analysis, expanded LF coverage, a fairer LLM baseline, and broader domain evaluation, this could become a solid Findings-track or workshop contribution. In its current form, it reads as a careful but incremental empirical study in a niche application domain."}],"totalDurationMs":124778,"totalTokens":87384,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":761,"cache_read_input_tokens":83769,"output_tokens":2851,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":761},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":2851,"cache_read_input_tokens":83769,"cache_creation_input_tokens":761,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":761,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WGtXJvnYBPYBFaseQRx7Fj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I now have both independent reviews. Let me proceed with the roundtable process:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews ✓ (done above)\nStep 2: Cross-share — I'll synthesize the cross-review perspectives\nStep 3: Converge — find agreement/disagreement\nStep 4: Final synthesis and submit\n\nLet me compose the full roundtable report now.\n\nLet me analyze the two reviews:\n\n**R1 (Methods + Experiments)**:\n- Soundness: 2/4, Contribution: 3/4, Presentation: 3/4, Overall: 3/6, Confidence: 4/5\n\n\n- Identifies the circular evaluation trap as a genuine contribution, appreciates the comprehensive multi-method comparison and failure taxonomy with honest negative results, but flags critical gaps: missing confidence intervals and significance tests, no inter-annotator agreement, underspecified gold set construction, weak LLM baseline, unclear cross-agent gold provenance, and no inter-coder reliability for the taxonomy itself.\n\n**R2 (Novelty + Related Work)**:\n- Soundness: 3/4, Contribution: 2/4, Presentation: 3/4, Overall: 3/6, Confidence: 4/5\n- Recognizes the quantified circular evaluation insight as a strength but sees limited novelty overall.\n\nBoth reviewers converge on the same critical issues: missing statistical rigor (significance tests, confidence intervals), absent inter-annotator agreement, and the missing null baseline for temporal onset alignment. They both value the circular evaluation framing and failure taxonomy, though they question the taxonomy's validity without inter-coder reliability. R2 also notes that the core finding isn't surprising to the weak supervision community and flags several missing related works including recent papers on label aggregation and LLM evaluation.\n\nThe key tension is that R1 worries more about experimental soundness (gold set provenance, LLM baseline fairness) while R2 emphasizes novelty and scope limitations. Both agree the paper could work as a workshop or findings paper but needs substantial revisions for a main venue: adding statistical tests, inter-annotator agreement, more labeling functions for the TLM experiments, broader domain coverage, the missing citations, and a fairer LLM baseline.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11144,"cache_read_input_tokens":49230,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11144},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b19c43c-1b27-4951-bda8-40543516a891","uuid":"a16f52fd-234a-4bf7-af50-1d9be79efdf8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WGtXJvnYBPYBFaseQRx7Fj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"两位 Reviewer 的独立评审已完成。进入 Roundtable 流程。\n\n## Step 2: Cross-Share\n\n**R1 → R2 传递的关键发现：**\n- OH 跨 agent gold 标签来源存疑（可能仍用 LF 标签而非真实人工标注）\n- 重训练实验（GB retrained on gold: 0.63）未做 learning curve 或交叉验证，无法区分数据不足 vs 特征瓶颈\n- LLM judge 仅用 zero-shot，未测试 few-shot/CoT，基线不公平\n\n**R2 → R1 传递的关键发现：**\n- 循环评估本身在 WS 文献中不算新发现（Snorkel 就建议 gold validation），本文贡献在于量化而非发现\n- 3 个 LF 太少，TLM 负面结果的泛化性存疑（WRENCH benchmark 通常 10+ LFs）\n- 缺少多个重要 related work（FlyingSquid, WRENCH, Safranchik et al., George et al. 2025）\n\n## Step 3: Convergence\n\n**共识点：**\n1. 缺少统计显著性检验/置信区间是最关键的方法论缺陷（两位 Reviewer 一致）\n2. Gold set 未报告 inter-annotator agreement，动摇所有基于 gold 的结论\n3. Temporal onset alignment 缺少随机基线\n4. 循环评估陷阱的量化展示是真实贡献，但不够新颖以支撑 top venue 主论文\n5. 失败分类法有价值但子类样本量小（n=35-82），无正式编码可靠性\n6. 论文适合 Findings track 而非主会议\n\n**分歧点：**\n- R1 对 soundness 评分更低（2/4 vs R2 的 3/4），因其关注实验执行细节\n- R2 对 contribution 评分更低（2/4 vs R1 的 3/4），因其关注新颖性\n- 综合来看两者结论一致：**Overall 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\n## Step 4: Final Synthesis\n\n现在生成最终审稿报告并提交。"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11144,"cache_read_input_tokens":49230,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11144},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b19c43c-1b27-4951-bda8-40543516a891","uuid":"9fd1f0b9-10c0-4cb9-9f3e-adeb95979504"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WGtXJvnYBPYBFaseQRx7Fj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PgmokT7sg6GoBJay9j6mD5","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: \"When Labeling Functions Lie: Circular Evaluation Traps in Weak Supervision for Agent Monitoring\"\n\n**Review Mode**: Full (2-Reviewer Roundtable)\n**Date**: 2026-05-14\n**Target Venue Standard**: Top ML venue (~25% acceptance, 6-point scale)\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer Assignment\n- **R1**: Methodology and Experiments\n- **R2**: Novelty and Related Work\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: Methodology and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThe paper identifies a \"circular evaluation trap\" in weak supervision for agent stagnation detection, demonstrating that structural features collapse from 0.995 to 0.55–0.61 F1 when evaluated on a 650-window gold set instead of LF-derived labels. A 5-category failure taxonomy and negative results on three TLM implementations are presented. The core insight is genuine and transferable, but the paper suffers from critical statistical gaps, an insufficiently validated gold set, and incomplete experimental analysis.\n\n### Strengths\n- **S1**: The circular evaluation trap is the paper's strongest contribution. The mechanistic explanation — structural features are continuous relaxations of LF inputs — is clean and generalizable to any WS pipeline where features correlate with label-generating heuristics.\n- **S2**: Comprehensive multi-method comparison on a shared gold set (Table 6) provides an apples-to-apples comparison across structural classifiers, neural probes, and LLM judges.\n- **S3**: The failure taxonomy (Table 8) is descriptively valuable, revealing that LFs handle only ~20% of gold stagnation (clear structural loops) while missing 73% of semantically diverse stagnation.\n- **S4**: Honest negative results on TLMs — three implementations failing, combined with the \"temporal alignment of noise is still noise\" insight.\n- **S5**: The convergence finding (all methods to 0.55–0.60 on gold) is genuinely surprising and underappreciated.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- **W1 (Critical)**: No confidence intervals, significance tests, or bootstrap analyses anywhere. The core claims rest on 1–5pp differences across methods on 650 samples (e.g., GB 0.58 vs. BGE 0.60 vs. LLM 0.56 in Table 6). Without McNemar's test or bootstrap 95% CIs, these differences may be noise. For an empirical analysis paper at a top venue, this is a disqualifying omission.\n- **W2 (Critical)**: No inter-annotator agreement (IAA) reported for the gold set. The paper's entire thesis rests on gold evaluation, yet the gold set's own reliability is uncharacterized. Without IAA ≥ 0.75, gold-derived numbers carry unknown measurement error.\n- **W3**: Gold set construction and sampling strategy are underspecified. How were 650 windows sampled? Was there stratification by confidence, trajectory length, agent type? Selection bias directly affects all conclusions.\n- **W4**: The retraining-on-gold experiment (GB: 0.63) conflates insufficient data with feature limitations. A learning curve or cross-validated estimate would be needed to disentangle the two.\n- **W5**: LLM judge baseline is unfairly weak — only zero-shot classification. No few-shot, no chain-of-thought, no alternative LLMs. The 2pp gap on gold could easily reverse with a fairer baseline.\n- **W6**: Cross-agent OH gold evaluation (Table 7, 0.52) — provenance of OH gold labels unclear. If these are LF-derived rather than human-annotated, this is a methodological inconsistency.\n- **W7**: Temporal onset alignment (28%, Table 10) lacks a random-firing null baseline for calibration.\n- **W8**: Failure taxonomy has no formal inter-coder reliability. Categories defined post-hoc by manual inspection without codebook or independent coding.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: Bootstrap 95% CIs for all pairwise comparisons in Table 6?\n- Q2: Inter-annotator agreement on the 650-window gold set? Single or multiple annotators?\n- Q3: How were 650 gold windows sampled? Any overlap with model training data?\n- Q4: Learning curve for gold-retrained GB (100/200/400/650 samples)?\n- Q5: LLM judge performance with 5-shot prompting and chain-of-thought?\n- Q6: OH cross-agent gold labels — human-annotated or LF-derived?\n- Q7: Random-firing null baseline for temporal onset alignment?\n- Q8: Error correlation analysis — do all methods fail on the same windows?\n\n### Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\n| # | Experiment | Status | Gap |\n|---|-----------|--------|-----|\n| 1 | LF-Gold Agreement | ✅ Covered | Missing IAA for gold itself |\n| 2 | Structural on Gold | ✅ Covered | Missing learning curve for retrained model |\n| 3 | Probe on Gold | ✅ Covered | Missing CIs |\n| 4 | LLM Judge on Gold | ✅ Covered | Zero-shot only; no few-shot/CoT |\n| 5 | Temporal Onset Alignment | ✅ Covered | Missing null baseline |\n| 6 | Failure Taxonomy | ✅ Covered | No inter-coder reliability |\n| 7 | Conf≥2 Reliability | ✅ Covered | Strong finding (37% error rate) |\n| 8 | Cross-Agent on Gold | ⚠️ Partial | OH gold provenance unclear |\n| 9 | F1-Stag Per-Subtype | ✅ Covered | Small N per category |\n| 10 | TLM Reinterpretation | ✅ Covered | Clean negative result |\n\n**Coverage**: ~85%. All 10 experiments are at least partially represented. Gaps are in statistical rigor and methodological documentation rather than missing experiments.\n\n### R1 Scores\n- Soundness: 2/4\n- Contribution: 3/4\n- Presentation: 3/4\n- Overall: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThe paper quantifies a circular evaluation trap in weak supervision for agent stagnation detection, showing all models converge to 0.55–0.60 F1 on human-annotated gold labels despite spanning 0.663–0.995 on LF-derived labels. While the quantification is careful and the failure taxonomy is concrete, the core finding is not surprising to the WS community, the scope is narrow (2 SE agents), and several important related works are missing.\n\n### Strengths\n- **S1**: The circular evaluation quantification is transferable — the 37–44pp collapse and the recommendation for feature-LF independence audits when LF-label F1 > 0.95 are actionable for practitioners.\n- **S2**: The 5-category failure taxonomy provides a concrete decomposition of where weak supervision breaks, with per-category recall numbers that guide future LF design.\n- **S3**: Well-structured TLM negative result. The \"temporal alignment of noise is still noise\" framing is accurate and memorable.\n- **S4**: The gold set validation is the right experimental design for addressing circularity. The paper practices what it preaches.\n- **S5**: Cross-agent analysis reveals non-obvious LF-architecture interactions (cmd_rep role reversal between SWE-Agent and OpenHands).\n\n### Weaknesses\n- **W1 (Major)**: The core finding — that features correlated with label-generating heuristics predict those labels — is not surprising to the WS community. The Snorkel papers explicitly discuss gold-set validation for exactly this reason. The contribution is a quantified case study, not a novel methodological insight. The \"all models collapse to 0.55–0.60\" convergence is the genuine surprise, but its generalizability beyond this setup is unclear.\n- **W2 (Major)**: Gold set limitations undermine the strongest claims. 650 windows, single domain, 2 agents, no IAA. The failure taxonomy subtypes have small N (n=35–82), limiting statistical power.\n- **W3**: No statistical significance tests on any comparison. If all methods converge to the same gold F1 range, this must be formally demonstrated.\n- **W4**: The TLM negative finding is expected given the experimental regime. Only 3 LFs with 95.1% agreement at conf≥2 — standard WS benchmarks use 10+ LFs. Cannot distinguish \"TLMs fail on sequential agent data\" from \"TLMs fail with 3 low-conflict LFs.\"\n- **W5**: Narrow scope. Title implies broad applicability to \"agent monitoring,\" but all evidence is from 2 coding agents on SWE-bench. Transfer to web navigation, scientific discovery, or conversational agents is unknown.\n- **W6**: No training on gold labels. Cross-validated gold-trained models would separate label quality bottleneck from model capacity bottleneck.\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n1. **Safranchik et al. (AAAI 2020)** — \"Weakly Supervised Sequence Tagging from Noisy Rules\": most direct methodological predecessor for the TLM approach (linked HMM for sequential WS). Critical omission.\n2. **Sala et al. (NeurIPS 2019)** — \"Multi-Resolution Weak Supervision for Sequential Data\": direct precedent for WS on sequential data with temporal structure.\n3. **Fu et al. (ICML 2020)** — FlyingSquid: core WS label model not compared against.\n4. **Zhang et al. (NeurIPS 2021)** — WRENCH benchmark: contextualizes the 3-LF regime as unusually small.\n5. **George et al. (2025)** — \"Unsupervised Cycle Detection in Agentic Applications\": most directly comparable work on stagnation/cycle detection combining structural and embedding approaches.\n6. **Lightman et al. (2023)** — \"Let's Verify Step by Step\": foundational PRM work missing from the PRM discussion.\n7. More sophisticated i.i.d. label models (Dawid-Skene EM, Snorkel's generative model, FlyingSquid) should be compared — MV is the simplest baseline, and the TLM negative finding is less informative without showing that sophisticated i.i.d. models also fail.\n\n### R2 Scores\n- Soundness: 3/4\n- Contribution: 2/4\n- Presentation: 3/4\n- Overall: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Discussion\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Reviewer Exchange\n\n**R1 → R2**: R1 flags that the OH cross-agent gold labels may not be true human annotations, which would mean Table 7's cross-agent collapse (0.967→0.52) is built on the same circular foundation the paper critiques. R1 also notes the LLM judge baseline is unfairly weak (zero-shot only), and the retraining-on-gold experiment conflates data insufficiency with feature limitations.\n\n**R2 → R1**: R2 notes that the circular evaluation finding, while well-quantified, is not novel to the WS community — Snorkel explicitly recommends gold validation. R2 identifies 7 significant missing references. R2 also argues the 3-LF regime makes the TLM negative result somewhat trivial — WRENCH benchmarks use 10+ LFs.\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\n\n**Points of consensus:**\n1. **Missing statistical rigor is the most critical flaw** — both reviewers independently flag the absence of CIs, significance tests, and bootstrap analyses as disqualifying for a top venue.\n2. **Missing inter-annotator agreement is a fundamental gap** — the paper's entire thesis rests on gold evaluation, yet the gold set's own reliability is uncharacterized.\n3. **Temporal onset alignment lacks a null baseline** — both flag this independently.\n4. **The circular evaluation trap quantification is a real contribution** — but better suited to Findings track than a main venue paper.\n5. **The failure taxonomy is valuable but undervalidated** — small N per category, no inter-coder reliability.\n6. **The paper is well-written and honestly reported** — both acknowledge presentation quality.\n\n**Points of divergence:**\n- R1 rates soundness lower (2/4) due to experimental execution gaps; R2 rates contribution lower (2/4) due to limited novelty. These are complementary concerns, not conflicting ones.\n\n**Reconciled assessment:** The paper identifies a genuine methodological issue (circular evaluation in WS for agent monitoring) and provides useful empirical evidence. However, (a) the core insight is not new to the WS community, (b) the experimental validation lacks statistical rigor, (c) the gold set foundation is insufficiently characterized, and (d) the scope is too narrow for the claims made. These issues place it below the acceptance threshold for a top ML venue main paper.\n\n---\n\n## Consolidated Assessment\n\n### Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n\n### Decision Rationale\nThe paper makes a genuine methodological contribution by quantifying the circular evaluation trap in weak supervision for agent monitoring. The 37–44pp collapse from LF-evaluation to gold-evaluation is a striking demonstration, and the failure taxonomy provides concrete guidance for future LF design. However, three fundamental issues prevent acceptance:\n\n1. **Statistical rigor**: No confidence intervals, significance tests, or bootstrap analyses on any comparison. Claims rest on 1–5pp differences over 650 samples.\n2. **Gold set validation**: No inter-annotator agreement, underspecified construction, unclear OH gold provenance.\n3. **Novelty threshold**: The circular evaluation concern is well-known in the WS literature. The contribution is a careful case study, not a novel insight.\n\n### Actionable Recommendations for Revision\n1. Add bootstrap 95% CIs and McNemar's tests for all pairwise model comparisons on gold.\n2. Report inter-annotator agreement (Cohen's κ) for the 650-window gold set with at least 2 annotators on a substantial subset.\n3. Include a random-firing null baseline for temporal onset alignment.\n4. Test LLM judge with few-shot prompting and chain-of-thought to establish a fair baseline.\n5. Expand to 6–8 LFs to provide a more informative test of temporal label models.\n6. Add learning curve for gold-retrained structural model to disentangle data insufficiency from feature limitation.\n7. Address all missing related work: Safranchik et al. (AAAI 2020), FlyingSquid (ICML 2020), WRENCH (NeurIPS 2021), George et al. (2025).\n8. Reframe scope in title/abstract to explicitly state this is about coding agents on SWE-bench, not general \"agent monitoring.\"\n9. Add error correlation analysis across methods to determine whether the 0.55–0.60 convergence reflects shared hard cases or complementary errors.\n10. Clarify gold set sampling strategy, potential contamination with training data, and OH gold label provenance.\n\n### Venue Recommendation\n- **Top ML venue (EMNLP/NeurIPS/ICML main)**: Below threshold in current form.\n- **Findings track (EMNLP Findings / ACL Findings)**: Strong candidate with statistical rigor added (items 1–3 above).\n- **Workshop paper (WS-specific workshop, e.g., Data-Centric AI)**: Acceptable as-is for a shorter format.\n\n### Score Summary\n\n| Criterion | R1 | R2 | Consolidated |\n|-----------|----|----|-------------|\n| Soundness | 2/4 | 3/4 | 2.5/4 |\n| Contribution | 3/4 | 2/4 | 2.5/4 |\n| Presentation | 3/4 | 3/4 | 3/4 |\n| Overall | 3/6 | 3/6 | **3/6 (Weak Reject)** |\n| Confidence | 4/5 | 4/5 | 4/5 |\n","project":"agent_progress_probe","tier":"worst","dream_id":"20260514T184058","overall_score":"3/6 Weak Reject","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11144,"cache_read_input_tokens":49230,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11144},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b19c43c-1b27-4951-bda8-40543516a891","uuid":"971b654d-c86a-4894-9c8b-dd17a76b49b1"}
